2100:
** εὐᾰρεστ-έω**,
**to be well pleasing**, τῷ Κοινῷ τῶν Δελφῶν {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 611.19 (2nd c.BC),cf. LXX.Gen.5.22, +others, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 14.4: _absolutely_, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.102; εὐαρέστησεν}}**it seemed good, it was resolved,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|Inscriptiones Graecae 14.757.8 (Naples, 1st c.AD)}}: —_passive_, **to be well pleased, satisfied**, τινι **with** a thing, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 55.23}}; τῇ νήσῳ, τῇ ἡγεμονίᾳ, {{NT+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 3.55, 20.79; θυσίαις NT.Heb.13.16.}}
**2** in Medicine texts, in _passive_, **to be benefited, get relief**, of patients, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Herodotus Medicus in “Rh.Mus.” 58.72,79.}}
**II** _intransitive_, = _passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysippus Comicus 7, 3rd-2nd c.BC: Apollonius Pergaeus Geometra “Conica” 1 “Praef.”, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 11.60, 5th c.AD: Hierocles Platonicus Philosophus “in Carmen Aureum - FPG” 11p.442M.}}

2101:
** εὐᾰρεστ-ος**, ον,
(ἀρέσκω) **wellpleasing, acceptable**, τὸ ἀγαθὸν εὐ. Cleanth. 3.6; τινι {{LXX+1st c.AD+|LXX.Wis.4.10, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.69, NT.2Cor.5.9, etc.}}; τισι {{Refs|“Ath.Mitt.” 15.134 (from Nisyrus)}}; παρά τινι {{LXX+1st c.BC+|LXX.Wis.9.10; ἐν τοῖς ἀναλώμασι “Inscription Prien.” 114.15 (1st c.BC): _absolutely_, ἀποδημία εὐ. 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.77; θέλημα τοῦ θεοῦ NT.Rom.12.2}}; χρῆσις **pleasant**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Herodotus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 5.27.20}}; σύμμαχοι probably in {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PHib.” 1.15.26 (_comparative_, 3rd c.BC)}}; τὸ εὐ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.585}}. _adverb_ -τως, ἔργον συνετέλεσεν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“IG” 12(8).640.10 (Peparethus, 2nd c.BC)}}: _comparative_ -οτέρως, διακεῖσθαί τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.5.5 (εὐαρεσκοτέρως codices)}}; - τως ἱερησάμενος {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 708.20 (Istropolis, 2nd c.BC),cf “IPE” 12.94 (from Olbia)}}; λατρεύειν τῷ θεῷ {{NT|NT.Heb.12.28.}}
**II** **choice**, οἶνος, πυρός, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“PStrassb.” 1.9 (6th c.AD), “PFlor.” 30.30 (4th c.AD).}}
**III** **according to taste**, λαχανόσπερμον λαμβάνειν εὐ. {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PFay.” 90.17 (3rd c.AD).}}

2102:
Included with: ** εὐᾰρεστ-ος**, ον,
(ἀρέσκω) **wellpleasing, acceptable**, τὸ ἀγαθὸν εὐ. Cleanth. 3.6; τινι {{LXX+1st c.AD+|LXX.Wis.4.10, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.69, NT.2Cor.5.9, etc.}}; τισι {{Refs|“Ath.Mitt.” 15.134 (from Nisyrus)}}; παρά τινι {{LXX+1st c.BC+|LXX.Wis.9.10; ἐν τοῖς ἀναλώμασι “Inscription Prien.” 114.15 (1st c.BC): _absolutely_, ἀποδημία εὐ. 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.77; θέλημα τοῦ θεοῦ NT.Rom.12.2}}; χρῆσις **pleasant**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Herodotus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 5.27.20}}; σύμμαχοι probably in {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PHib.” 1.15.26 (_comparative_, 3rd c.BC)}}; τὸ εὐ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.585}}. _adverb_ -τως, ἔργον συνετέλεσεν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“IG” 12(8).640.10 (Peparethus, 2nd c.BC)}}: _comparative_ -οτέρως, διακεῖσθαί τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.5.5 (εὐαρεσκοτέρως codices)}}; - τως ἱερησάμενος {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 708.20 (Istropolis, 2nd c.BC),cf “IPE” 12.94 (from Olbia)}}; λατρεύειν τῷ θεῷ {{NT|NT.Heb.12.28.}}
**II** **choice**, οἶνος, πυρός, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“PStrassb.” 1.9 (6th c.AD), “PFlor.” 30.30 (4th c.AD).}}
**III** **according to taste**, λαχανόσπερμον λαμβάνειν εὐ. {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PFay.” 90.17 (3rd c.AD).}}

2104:
** εὐγεν-ής**, ές,
in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} **εὐηγενής** (which see), and in {{Refs|“hymnus ad Venerem” 94}} **ἠϋγενής**: (γένος):— **well-born**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 704 (troch.), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 728, etc.; εὐ. δόμος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1540}}; τὸ μὲν ἐστίχθαι εὐγενὲς κέκριται being tattooed is esteemed **a mark of nobility**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.6.}}
**2** in Trag. etc. with the connotation **noble-minded, generous** (more properly γενναῖος,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1390b22), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 38, “Ph.” 874, etc.}}; διαφέρει φύσις γενναίου σκύλακος.. νεανίσκου εὐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 375a.}}
**3** of animals, **high-bred**, ἵππος {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 184, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 25; λέων 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1259; ὄρνιθες 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.58.7}}; of plants, **of a good sort**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 2.14; ῥόαι 4th c.BC: Eriphus Comicus 2.11; πυροί 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 11.120; βλαστοί “Geoponica” 5.37.2}}: so in _comparative_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 44; φλέβες καὶ ἶνες 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 5.1.7 (assuming variant, compare εὐτενής); χαλκός 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 864 (variant)}}: _metaphorically_, of a wife, ὥσπερ εὐγενῆ χώραν ἐντεκνώσασθαι παρασχεῖν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Mi.” 25.}}
**4** of outward form, **noble**, δέρη, πρόσωπον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 136, “Med.” 1072}}; of style, τὸ εὐ. τῆς λέξεως {{Refs 1st c.BC+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “NA Epil.”; εὐ. ῥυθμοί 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 18.}}
**II** _adverb_ -νῶς **nobly, bravely**, κατθανοῦμεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 201, compare “Tr.” 727; εὐτυχεῖν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.7f.}}

2105:
** εὐδία**,
_Ionic dialect_ -ιη, ἡ, **fair weather**, εὐδία ἐκ χειμῶνος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 7(6).38, compare 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.2.1, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Insomn.” 89; ἐν εὐδίᾳ χειμῶνα ποιεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.4.14; ὅταν εὐ. γένηται 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 551a3}}; εὐδίας (_genitive_) **in fine weather**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 597b13: _plural_, ἔν γε χειμῶσιν καὶ ἐν εὐδίαις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 961e; εὐδιῶν οὐσῶν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 626a4.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **tranquillity, peace**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.98, “P.” 5.10, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 795, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.8.20; τὴν Αἴγυπτον εἰς εὐδίαν ἀγαγεῖν “OGI” 90.11 (Rosetta, 2nd c.BC),compare 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 1.28; εὐ. καὶ διαγωγὴ ἄλυπος 3rd c.BC: Polystratus Epicureus p.17}} W.; of the mind, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Protagoras Philosophus 9}}; σαρκὸς εὐ. **good condition** of.., {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.126c}}; εἰς ἔμ᾽ εὐδίαν ἔχων being at **ease** so far as I am concerned, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ichn.” 346. [On the prosody, see at {εὔδιος}.]}}]

2106:
** εὐδοκ-έω**,
**to be well pleased** or **content**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.49.2, +others}}; ἐάν.. {{Refs|[prev. author] 29.12.8}}: with _participle_, {{Refs|[prev. author] 15.36.6}}: with _dative_, **to be content with, find pleasure in** a person or thing, {{NT+1st c.BC+|[prev. author] 18.52.5, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.47, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 8.74; τῇ ἀδικίᾳ NT.2Thes.2.12; ἔν τινι LXX.Isa.62.4, +others, NT.2Cor.12.10, +others; εἴς τινα NT.2Pet.1.17; ἐπί τινι LXX.Jdt.15.10}}: also with _accusative_, τινα{{LXX|LXX.Gen.33.10}}; [ἐγγύους] {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 672.27 (Delph., 2nd c.BC)}}; ἡ γῆ -ήσει τὰ σάββατα {{LXX|LXX.Lev.26.34.}}
**2** **consent, approve**, with _dative_, τοῖς γεγραμμένοις {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PLond.” 3.1168.15 (1st c.AD), +others}}; τῇ δημοσιώσει {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1273.40 (3rd c.AD)}}; also ἐπί τινι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PTeb.” 317.33 (2nd c.AD), +others}}: frequently _absolutely_ in legal documents, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PRyl.” 120.24 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}
**3** with _infinitive_, **consent, agree to do**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.78.8, +others}} (and with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, **consent** that.., {{LXX|[prev. author] 1.8.4, LXX.2Mac.14.35)}}; **to be ready, willing,** {{NT+2nd c.BC+|PGrenf. 1.1.17 (2nd c.BC), NT.1Thes.2.8.}}
**3.b** **determine, resolve, NT.Luke.**{{Refs|12.32, etc.}}
**4** **to be content, happy**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 1.1}}:— also in _middle_, τῆς εὐδοκουμένης ζωῆς {{Refs|[prev. author] “Mort.” 36.}}
**5** _middle_, = _active_ in _signification_{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|1, ἐπί τινι 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.8.4}}; τινι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. author] 3.31.6, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 15.16 codices; περί τινος 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.Supp.” p.44S.}}
**II** _passive_, **to be favoured**, i.e. **prosper**, {{LXX|LXX.1Ch.29.23}}: with _dative_, **find approval with**, τισι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.88.4, +others}}
**II.2** **to be approved**, ὑπό τινων {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“BGU” 1157.12 (1st c.BC).}}
**III** of persons or things, **to be well-pleasing** or **acceptable, find favour with**, τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 20.5.10, 2nd c.AD: Maximus Tyrius Sophista 32.5; τὰ -οῦντα ἑαυτῷ 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.Supp.” p.54 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}
**IV** with _accusative_ et _genitive_, **deem worthy of**, τινα τιμῆς {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PLond.” 1.3.6 (2nd c.BC).}}

2107:
** εὐδοκ-ία**, ἡ,
={εὐδόκησις}, especially of God, {{LXX+NT|LXX.1Ch.16.10, +others, NT.Luke.2.14, +others}}; **good will,** {{NT|NT.Phil.1.15}}; **contentment**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Pietate - Herkulanische Studien” 25.}}
**2** **object of desire**, {{LXX|LXX.Psa.144.16, LXX.Sir.18.31.}}
**3** variant for{εὐδοκιμίη} in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “παραγγελίαι” 6.}}

2108:
** εὐεργ-εσία**,
_Ionic dialect_ **εὐεργ-εσίη**, ἡ, **well-doing**, opposed to κακοεργίη, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.374}}; opposed to κακότης, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 548, etc.}}
**II** **a good deed, kindness**, εὐεργεσίας ἀποτίνειν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.235, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 503 (_plural_)}}; ἡ ἐξ Ἱστιαίου εὐ. done by him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.11; ἐκτίνειν [prev. author] 3.47 (_plural_)}}; εὐεργεσίας ἀποδέξασθαι ἔς τινας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 67; καταθέσθαι ἔς τινα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.128; εὐ. πεποιημέναι ἔς τινα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.165; προέσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.7.47; προσφέρειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 513e; opposed to εὐ. ἀπολαβεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 14.57; εὐ. ὀφείλεταί μοι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.137, compare 32}}; ἀντ᾽ εὐεργεσίης for **service done**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 97.6, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 17.116, compare 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 1.47 (_plural_), “IG” 12.108; ἀπ᾽ εὐεργεσίας καθίστασαν τοὺς βασιλεῖς 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1286b10: with _genitive_, εὐ. τῆς πόλεως}} **good service done** the state, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 850b}}: _plural_, **public services**, τὰς τῶν προγόνων εὐεργεσίας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 14.24, etc.}}
**II.2** ψηφίζεσθαί [τινι] εὐεργεσίαν to vote him **the title of** εὐεργέτης (which see), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.60, compare “IG” 22.29, etc.; κείσεταί σοι εὐ. ἐν τῷ ἡμετέρῳ οἴκῳ ἐς αἰεὶ ἀνάγραπτος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.129, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.1.26, etc.}}
**III** Εὐεργεσία, personified, = Latin {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Liberalitas”, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 71.34.}}
**III.2** epithet of Hera at Argos, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}

2109:
** εὐεργετ-έω**,
the augment εὐηργ- is sometimes found, especially in codices, as _imperfect_ εὐηργέτουν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Apologia Socratis” 26}}: _aorist_ εὐηργέτησα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 835, variant in 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.16, but ηὐεργέτησα 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 9.14:}}_perfect_ εὐηργέτηκα variant in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 140}}: _perfect_ _passive_ εὐηργέτημαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.2.3, “SIG” 798.5 (Cyzicus, 1st c.AD)}}, but Inscrr. and Papyrus have εὐεργέτηκα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 22.573 (4th c.BC), εὐεργέτημαι “PLond.” 2.169.26 (1st c.AD), εὐεργετήθην “IG” 7.2808 (Hyettus, 3rd c.AD), ηὐεργετημένοι “PTeb.” 326.16 (3rd c.AD)}}:—**to be a benefactor**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 670, “IG” 22.786, etc.; [Ἰησοῦς] διῆλθεν -τῶν NT.Act.10.38.}}
**2** **to be proclaimed as** εὐεργέτης {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1.2, “JHS” 10.76 (Patara, 1st c.AD).}}
**II** with _accusative_ _person_, **do good services** or **show kindness to** one, τοὺς θανόντας εἰ θέλεις εὐεργετεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 266.1, compare “Eu.” 725, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1540, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator [same place], etc.; ὁ νόμος βούλεται -τεῖν βίον ἀνθρώπων 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 248; εὐ. τὸν δῆμον “IG” 22.791.25, etc.; τὸν θεὸν εὐεργετηκότες “SIG” 417.13 (Delph., 3rd c.BC)}}: with _accusative_ _cognate_, εὐ. τινὰ τὴν μεγίστην εὐεργεσίαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 36c, compare “R.” 615b; ὅ τι ἂν ἡμᾶς εὐεργετήσῃς [prev. work] 345a}}; μεγάλως or μεγάλα εὐ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.2.10, 12: with _dative_ _of things_, χρήμασιν εὐ. [prev. work] 2}}:—_passive_, **have a kindness done one**, εὐεργεσίαν εὐεργετηθείς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 520c; μείζω εὐηργετημένοι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.2.3; καί τι εὐεργέτηται ὑπ᾽ ἐμοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 43a; ἀντὶ πολλῶν καὶ μεγάλων ὧν εὐεργετήθη παρὰ τοῦ θεοῦ “IG” 7.2808 (Hyettus, 3rd c.AD)}}; εὐεργετούμενος εἰς χρήματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 184b.}}

2110:
** εὐεργέτ-ης**
(Thess. **εὐϝεργέτας** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 9(2).257.5 (5th c.BC))}}, ου, ὁ, **benefactor**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.24, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 284}}; τινι to one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.30, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1252}}: more commonly with _genitive_, τῆς γῆς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Rh.” 151, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 403e, etc.}}
**2** as an honorary title, εὐ. βασιλέος ἀνεγράφη was registered as the King's **benefactor**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.85, compare 3.140}}; πρόξεινος καὶ εὐ. {{NT+5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 8.136, compare “IG” 12.82, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.1.4, etc.; μέγιστος εὐ. παρ᾽ ἐμοὶ ἀναγεγράψῃ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 506c, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 20.19, etc.; οἱ ἐξουσιασταὶ αὐτῶν εὐ. καλοῦνται NT.Luke.22.25}}: conferred on kings and emperors, as Antigonus, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Inscription Prien.” 2.6 (4th c.BC)}}; ὁ παντὸς κόσμου σωτὴρ καὶ εὐ, of Trajan, {{Refs|“IG” 12(1).978 (from Carpathos)}}; σὺ ὁ εὐ., mode of address to a superior, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“POxy.” 38.13 (1st c.AD), 486.27 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}
**II** as _adjective_, **beneficent, bountiful**, ἀνήρ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.94, compare “P.” 4.30.}}

2111:
** εὔθετ-ος**, ον,
(τίθημι) **well-arranged, conveniently placed**, ὀστέα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “κατ᾽ ἰητρεῖον” 15}}: _comparative_ -ώτερος {{Refs|[prev. author] “Fract.” 4}}; ἐν εὐ. τόπῳ in a **suitable** place, Ἀρχ.Δελτ.{{Refs|7.200 (from Ephesus). b. of the ashes of a corpse}}**easily stowed**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 444 (Lyric poetry)}}; so of the corpse, **laid out** for burial (compare εὐθετέω), {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“Supp.epigram” 1.449 (Phrygia, 3rd c.AD)}}; εὔ. σάκος, ἀρβύλαι, **well-fitting, ready for use**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 642 (Scholia, εὔκυκλον codex _middle_), “fragment” 259; εὔ. εἴς τι 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 2.57; πρός τι [prev. author] 5.37}}; εὔθετόν ἐστι with _infinitive_, it is **convenient**.., {{Refs|[prev. author] 21.21}}; καιρὸς εὔ. {{LXX+1st c.BC+|LXX.Psa.31(32).6, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 5.57.}}
**2** of persons, **well-adapted**, εἰς τοὺς τραγῳδοὺς εὔ., οὐκ εἰς τὸν βίον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 105.5}}; εἰς, πρὸς φιλίαν, {{NT+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.46 W., “Lib.” p.45 O.; εἰς τὴν βασιλείαν τοῦ θεοῦ NT.Luke.9.62}}; πράγματι for a business, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|Nicolaus Rhetor cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 3.1.40; πρός τι 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 25.3.6, etc.}}; **quick, able**, κατὰ τὰς ἐντεύξεις τοῖς ὄχλοις εὔ. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 33.22: _absolutely_, εὔθετοι}}**fit and proper persons,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|PTeb. 27.44 (2nd c.BC)}}, etc. _adverb_ -τως, ἔχειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 23; πρός τι 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 33.4.}}

2112:
Included with: ** εὐθύς**, εῖα, ύ,
_Ionic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ **ἰθύς** (which see: so always in Hom.and {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus)}},
**A** **straight, direct**, whether vertically or horizontally, opposed to σκολιός, καμπύλος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 194b, “R.” 602c, etc.}}; κατὰ τὸ εὐθὺ ἑστάναι stands still with reference to **the vertical**, of a spinning top, {{Refs|[prev. work] 436e}}; εὐ. πλόος, ὁδοί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.103, “N.” 1.25, etc.; εὐθυτέρα ὁδός 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.4; ὁδοὺς εὐθείας ἔτεμε 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.100; ῥόμβος ἀκόντων 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.93}}; εὐθείᾳ (i.e. ὁδῷ) **by the straight road**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 716a; εὐθεῖαν ἕρπε 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 195}}; τὴν εὐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 384; ἐπ᾽ εὐθείας 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 19.38, 1st c.BC: Asclepiodotus Tacticus “Tact.” 2.6, 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 2.1.8; so also εἰς τὸ εὐ.βλέπειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 7.17, etc.}}; πλήρης τοῦ εὐθέος tired **of going straight forward**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 14}}; ἡ ἐς τὸ εὐ. τῆς ῥητορικῆς ὁδός the **direct** road to.., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Rh.Pr.” 10}}; κατ᾽ εὐθύ on **level ground**, {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.21.23}}; but ἡ κατ᾽ εὐ. τάσις **in the direct line**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Apollonius Citiensis Medicus 2}}; **on the same side**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 8.62}}; also, opposed to εἰς τὸ ἐντός, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.7.14.}}
**A.2** in moral sense, **straightforward, frank**, of persons, εὐθὺν χρὴ τὸν ἑταῖρον ἔμμεν καὶ μὴ σκολιὰ φρονεῖν {{Refs 7th c.BC+|“Scol.” 16; κοινᾶνι παρ᾽ εὐθυτάτῳ 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.28; ῥῆτραι 7th c.BC: Tyrtaeus Elegiacus 4.6; τόλμα 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.12; δίκα [prev. author] “N.” 10.12; κρῖνε δ᾽ εὐθεῖαν δίκην 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 433, compare Ἀρχ. Ἐφ. 1911.134 (from Gonni)}}; ὁ εὐθὺς λόγος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 492; τὸ εὐ. τε καὶ τὸ ἐλεύθερον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 173a}}; ἀπὸ τοῦ εὐθέος λέγειν to speak **straight out**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.43}}; ἐκ τοῦ εὐ. ὑπουργεῖν **outright, openly, without reserve**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.34}}; ἐκ τοῦ εὐ., opposed to δι᾽ αἰνιγμάτων, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 8.8.3: in _feminine_, τὴν εὐθεῖάν τινι συνειπεῖν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cicero” 7; ἁπλῶς καὶ δι᾽ εὐθείας [prev. author] 2.408e}}; ἀπ᾽ εὐθείας {{Refs|[prev. work]57a, “Fab.” 3}}; κατ᾽ εὐθεῖαν **by direct reasoning**, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 432}}; μηδὲν ἐξ εὐθείας παρέχει (an amulet) does no good **directly**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.42.}}
**A.3** εὐθεῖα, ἡ, as _substantive_,
**A.3.a** (i.e. γραμμή) **straight line**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Priora” 49b35, +others, 3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 1 “Def.” 7, +others}}; ἐπ᾽ εὐθείας εἶναι lie in a **straight line**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ κωνοειδέων καὶ σφαιροειδέων” 7, +others}}; ἐπὶ τὴν αὐτὴν εὐ., ἐπὶ τῆς αὐτῆς εὐ. ἐκτείνειν, in the same **line**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.113.2, 3; ἐπὶ μίαν εὐ. [prev. work] 8}}: _comparative_, εὐθυτέρα ἡ γραμμὴ γίνεται {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mechanica” 855a24.}}
**A.3.b** (i.e. πτῶσις) **nominative case**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus 636.5, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 6.11, etc.}}; κατ᾽ εὐθύ in **the nominative**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Sophistici Elenchi” 182a3.}}
**B** as _adverb_, **εὐθύς** and **εὐθύ**, the former properly of Time, the latter of Place, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 119, etc.}}
**B.I** **εὐθύ**, of Place, **straight**, usually of motion or direction, εὐθὺ Πύλονδε **straight to..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|hymnus ad Mercurium 342; εὐθὺ πρὸς τὰ νυμφικὰ λέχη 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1242}}; εὐ. [τὴν ἐπὶ] Βαβυλῶνος **straight towards**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.2.37: and so with _genitive_, εὐ. τῶν κυρηβίων, εὐθὺ Πελλήνης, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 254, “Av.” 1421; εὐ.τοῦ Διός [prev. author] “Pax” 68; εὐ. τοὐρόφου 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 47; εὐ. τῆς σωτηρίας 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 301, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.88, etc.}}; ἀποθανούμενος ᾔει εὐ. τοῦ δαιμονίου **in opposition to**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theages” 129a (assuming variant)}}; compare ἰθύς.
**B.I.b** νῆσον οἰκεῖ εὐθὺ Ἴστρου **opposite**.., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Maximus Tyrius Sophista 15.7.}}
**B.I.2** ={ἁπλῶς}, **simply**, καλεῖν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 3.8.2, compare 9.13.2.}}
**B.I.3** rarely of Time, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Philochorus Historicus 144, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1414b25, “UPZ” 77.27 (2nd c.BC), “PGrenf.” 1.1.24 (2nd c.BC), Aristeas 24, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nav.” 22.}}
**B.II** **εὐθύς**,
**B.II.1** of Time, **straightway, forthwith**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 8.41; ὁ δ᾽ εὐ. ὡς ἤκουσε 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 361; ὁ δ᾽ εὐ. ἐξῴμωξεν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 317; τὸ μὲν εὐ. τὸ δὲ καὶ διανοούμενον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.1, compare 5.3, 7.77}}; joined with other adverbial words, τάχα δ᾽ εὐ. ἰών {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.83; εὐ. κατὰ τάχος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.101}}; εὐ. παραχρῆμα (see. entry παραχρῆμα); εὐ. ἀπ᾽ αρχῆς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 84 (_anapaest meter_); εὐ. ἐξ ἀρχῆς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.2.16}}; ἐξ ἀρχῆς εὐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1287b10; εὐ. κατ᾽ ἀρχάς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 24b; ἀφ᾽ ἑσπέρας εὐ. ἤδη 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Gall.” 1}}; εὐ. ἐκ νέου, ἐκ παιδός, **even** from one's youth, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 485d, 519a; εὐ. ἐκ παιδίου 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.20}}: with a _participle_, εὐ. νέοι ὄντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.39; εὐ. ἥκων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.7.2; εὐ. ἀπεκτονώς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.127}}; τοῦ θέρους εὐ. ἀρχομένου **just** at the beginning of summer, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.47}}; ἀρξάμενος εὐ. καθισταμένου [τοῦ πολέμου] from the **very** beginning of the war, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.1}}; εὐ. ἀποβεβηκότι **immediately** on disembarking, {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.43}}; εὐ. γενομένοις at **the moment of** birth, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 186b}}: _metaphorically_, **at once, naturally**, ὑπάρχει εὐθὺς γένη ἔχον τὸ ὄν Being falls **at once** into genera, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1004a5, compare “Po.” 1452a14}}: with _substantive_, ἡ τῶν Ἰταλιωτῶν εὐθὺς φυγή {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 8.1.5.}}
**B.II.2** less frequently in a local relation, ὑπὲρ τῆς πόλεως εὐ. **just** above the city, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.96}}; παρ᾽ αὐτὴν εὐ. ὁ ἔσπλους ἐστίν **directly** past it (the mole), {{Refs|[prev. author] 8.90}}; ἐγγύτατα τούτου εὐ. ἐχομένη **immediately** adjoining this, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. passage],compare 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 25.23}}; εὐ. ἐπὶ τὴν γέφυραν Foed. cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.118, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.2.1, 2, 2.4.24, “Ages.” 1.29}}; τὴν εὐ. Ἄργους κἀπιδαυρίας ὁδόν the road **leading straight to** Argos, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1197 (condemned by 9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc.); εὐ. Λυκείου 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 110, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 498a32, etc.}}
**B.II.3** of Manner, **directly, simply**, variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 100a.}}
**B.II.4** like{αὐτίκα} {{Refs|11:}}**for instance, to take the first example that occurs**, ὥσπερ ζῷον εὐθύς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1277a6, compare “Cael.” 284b10, etc.; οἷον εὐθύς 2nd c.AD: Cleomedes Astronomus 1.1, 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 11.145.}}
**C** regular _adverb_ **εὐθέως**, used just as εὐθύς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 31, “OC” 994, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 31, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 63a, etc.}}; αἰσθόμενος εὐθέως **as soon as** he perceived, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.11; ἐπεὶ εὐθέως ᾔσθοντο 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.2.4; εὐθέως παραχρῆμα 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.20, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 52.6.}}
**C.2** ={εὐθύς} {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 11.4, οἷον εὐθέως}} as **for example**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.52.1, 12.5.6 (uncertain meaning in 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 55)}}; so εὐ. alone, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.589. (εὐθέως}} is the commoner form in later Greek, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 34.17 (3rd c.BC), etc.)}}

2114:
** εὐθῡμ-έω**,
**to be of good cheer**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 530, “Anthologia Graeca” 5.100, NT.Jam.5.13}}: _feminine_ _dative_ _plural_ _present_ _participle_ εὐθυμεύσαις {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.143}}; περὶ τῆς κριθῆς εὐθύμει **do not be anxious** about.., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PAmh.” 2.133.4 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II** _transitive_, **cheer, delight**, τινα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 350.4, compare 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 279}}:—_passive_, **to be cheerful**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 3, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.4.36; ἐπί τινι [prev. author] “Cyr.” 4.1.19; ἐν ταῖς ἀτυχίαις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1379b18; πάντα-εῖσθαι χρή 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “ὀξέων νούσων θεραπευτικόν” 1.1.}}

2115:
** εὔθῡμ-ος**, ον,
**kind, generous**, ἄναξ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.63.}}
**II** **cheerful**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 174, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.4.13 (_comparative_), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 792b}}; συμπόσια εὔ.{{Refs 5th c.BC+|Ion Eleg. 1.14; φέρειν γῆρας εὔ. εἰς τελευτάν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 5.22}}; of horses, **spirited**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 11.12 (_superlative_)}}; τὸ εὔ., ={εὐθυμία}, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1106c, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 42.1}}. _adverb_ -μως **cheerfully**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|Batrachomyomachia 159, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1592}}: _comparative_ -ότερον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.2.27}}: _superlative_ -ότατα {{Refs|[prev. work] 3.3.12.}}

2116:
** εὐθῡν-ω**,
(εὐθύς) = the Homeric ἰθύνω (which is a frequently variant, as in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 773)}}: — **guide straight, direct**, οἰωνὸν γνώμῃ στομίων ἄτερ εὐθύνων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pr.” 289; εὐ. ἡνίας 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1739 (Lyric poetry)}}; [ἅρματα] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.32}}; εὐ. δόρυ **steer** the bark **straight**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 15; εὐ. πλάτην [prev. author] “Hec.” 39; ὁ εὐθύνων}}**the helmsman,** {{NT|NT.Jam.3.4}}; εὐ. ἀγέλας **lead** or **drive** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.1.2}}; εὐ. χερσί **manage, guide** him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 542; εὐ. πόδα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 728, etc.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **direct, govern**, Κύρου δὲ παῖς.. ηὔθυνε στρατόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 773; πᾶσαν εὐθύνων πόλιν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 178, compare 1164, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 9, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Minos” 320d.}}
**3** **make straight, straighten**, opposed to κάμπτειν, πτέρυγας {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Incessu Animalium” 709b10}}:— _passive_, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Mete.” 385b32.}}
**II** **make** or **put straight**, εὐ. δίκας σκολιάς **make** crooked judgements **straight**, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Solon Lyricus 4.37; εὐ. λαοῖς δίκας 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.153}}; εὐ. οὖρον **send a straight** fair wind, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “O.” 13.28; εὐ. ὄλβον [prev. author] “P.” 1.46; ὥσπερ ξύλον διαστρεφόμενον.. εὐ. ἀπειλαῖς καὶ πληγαῖς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 325d, compare 326e.}}
**III** **examine the conduct of** an official, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Plt.” 299a; εὐ. τὰς ἀρχάς 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1271a6, 1274a17, +others}}:—_passive_, with _genitive_, - όμενος τῆς ἐφορείας {{Refs|[prev. author] “Rh.” 1419a31.}}
**III.2** with _genitive_ criminis, **call to account for**.., τινα κλοπῆς {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cicero” 9:}} —_passive_, τῶν ἀδικημάτων ηὐθύνθη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.95}}; οἱ -όμενοι **the culprits**, Mitteis {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Chr.” 31 iii 10 (2nd c.BC),compare “Notiz.Arch.” 4.21 (Cyrene, 1st c.BC)}}: with _dative_, εὐ. φόνῳ {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 14.4 (2nd c.BC)}}; **to be mulcted, punished**, ἐπί τινι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus “fragment” 90, +others: _absolutely_, “IG” 12.41.6; ἑκατὸν δραχμῆσι [prev. work]4.15, +others}}
**III.3** generally, **refute** or **censure**, τοὺς λόγους τινός {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Pietate - Herkulanische Studien” 67; τὴν Φιλίστου διάλεκτον 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Nicias” 1}}:—_passive_, **to be refuted**, δι᾽ αὐτῶν τῶν φαινομένων {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 30, compare 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 3.6.13}}; but also, **to be critically examined**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.6.13.}}
**III.4** **examine by torture**, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Procopius Caesariensis Historicus “de Bello Gothico” 3.32 (from Pass).}}
**IV** _intransitive_, **serve as** εὔθυνος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 946d.}}

2117:
** εὐθύς**, εῖα, ύ,
_Ionic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ **ἰθύς** (which see: so always in Hom.and {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus)}},
**A** **straight, direct**, whether vertically or horizontally, opposed to σκολιός, καμπύλος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 194b, “R.” 602c, etc.}}; κατὰ τὸ εὐθὺ ἑστάναι stands still with reference to **the vertical**, of a spinning top, {{Refs|[prev. work] 436e}}; εὐ. πλόος, ὁδοί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.103, “N.” 1.25, etc.; εὐθυτέρα ὁδός 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.4; ὁδοὺς εὐθείας ἔτεμε 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.100; ῥόμβος ἀκόντων 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.93}}; εὐθείᾳ (i.e. ὁδῷ) **by the straight road**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 716a; εὐθεῖαν ἕρπε 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 195}}; τὴν εὐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 384; ἐπ᾽ εὐθείας 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 19.38, 1st c.BC: Asclepiodotus Tacticus “Tact.” 2.6, 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 2.1.8; so also εἰς τὸ εὐ.βλέπειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 7.17, etc.}}; πλήρης τοῦ εὐθέος tired **of going straight forward**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 14}}; ἡ ἐς τὸ εὐ. τῆς ῥητορικῆς ὁδός the **direct** road to.., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Rh.Pr.” 10}}; κατ᾽ εὐθύ on **level ground**, {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.21.23}}; but ἡ κατ᾽ εὐ. τάσις **in the direct line**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Apollonius Citiensis Medicus 2}}; **on the same side**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 8.62}}; also, opposed to εἰς τὸ ἐντός, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.7.14.}}
**A.2** in moral sense, **straightforward, frank**, of persons, εὐθὺν χρὴ τὸν ἑταῖρον ἔμμεν καὶ μὴ σκολιὰ φρονεῖν {{Refs 7th c.BC+|“Scol.” 16; κοινᾶνι παρ᾽ εὐθυτάτῳ 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.28; ῥῆτραι 7th c.BC: Tyrtaeus Elegiacus 4.6; τόλμα 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.12; δίκα [prev. author] “N.” 10.12; κρῖνε δ᾽ εὐθεῖαν δίκην 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 433, compare Ἀρχ. Ἐφ. 1911.134 (from Gonni)}}; ὁ εὐθὺς λόγος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 492; τὸ εὐ. τε καὶ τὸ ἐλεύθερον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 173a}}; ἀπὸ τοῦ εὐθέος λέγειν to speak **straight out**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.43}}; ἐκ τοῦ εὐ. ὑπουργεῖν **outright, openly, without reserve**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.34}}; ἐκ τοῦ εὐ., opposed to δι᾽ αἰνιγμάτων, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 8.8.3: in _feminine_, τὴν εὐθεῖάν τινι συνειπεῖν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cicero” 7; ἁπλῶς καὶ δι᾽ εὐθείας [prev. author] 2.408e}}; ἀπ᾽ εὐθείας {{Refs|[prev. work]57a, “Fab.” 3}}; κατ᾽ εὐθεῖαν **by direct reasoning**, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 432}}; μηδὲν ἐξ εὐθείας παρέχει (an amulet) does no good **directly**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 2.42.}}
**A.3** εὐθεῖα, ἡ, as _substantive_,
**A.3.a** (i.e. γραμμή) **straight line**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Analytica Priora” 49b35, +others, 3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 1 “Def.” 7, +others}}; ἐπ᾽ εὐθείας εἶναι lie in a **straight line**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ κωνοειδέων καὶ σφαιροειδέων” 7, +others}}; ἐπὶ τὴν αὐτὴν εὐ., ἐπὶ τῆς αὐτῆς εὐ. ἐκτείνειν, in the same **line**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.113.2, 3; ἐπὶ μίαν εὐ. [prev. work] 8}}: _comparative_, εὐθυτέρα ἡ γραμμὴ γίνεται {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mechanica” 855a24.}}
**A.3.b** (i.e. πτῶσις) **nominative case**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus 636.5, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 6.11, etc.}}; κατ᾽ εὐθύ in **the nominative**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Sophistici Elenchi” 182a3.}}
**B** as _adverb_, **εὐθύς** and **εὐθύ**, the former properly of Time, the latter of Place, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 119, etc.}}
**B.I** **εὐθύ**, of Place, **straight**, usually of motion or direction, εὐθὺ Πύλονδε **straight to..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|hymnus ad Mercurium 342; εὐθὺ πρὸς τὰ νυμφικὰ λέχη 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1242}}; εὐ. [τὴν ἐπὶ] Βαβυλῶνος **straight towards**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.2.37: and so with _genitive_, εὐ. τῶν κυρηβίων, εὐθὺ Πελλήνης, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 254, “Av.” 1421; εὐ.τοῦ Διός [prev. author] “Pax” 68; εὐ. τοὐρόφου 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 47; εὐ. τῆς σωτηρίας 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 301, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.88, etc.}}; ἀποθανούμενος ᾔει εὐ. τοῦ δαιμονίου **in opposition to**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theages” 129a (assuming variant)}}; compare ἰθύς.
**B.I.b** νῆσον οἰκεῖ εὐθὺ Ἴστρου **opposite**.., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Maximus Tyrius Sophista 15.7.}}
**B.I.2** ={ἁπλῶς}, **simply**, καλεῖν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 3.8.2, compare 9.13.2.}}
**B.I.3** rarely of Time, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Philochorus Historicus 144, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1414b25, “UPZ” 77.27 (2nd c.BC), “PGrenf.” 1.1.24 (2nd c.BC), Aristeas 24, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nav.” 22.}}
**B.II** **εὐθύς**,
**B.II.1** of Time, **straightway, forthwith**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 8.41; ὁ δ᾽ εὐ. ὡς ἤκουσε 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 361; ὁ δ᾽ εὐ. ἐξῴμωξεν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 317; τὸ μὲν εὐ. τὸ δὲ καὶ διανοούμενον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.1, compare 5.3, 7.77}}; joined with other adverbial words, τάχα δ᾽ εὐ. ἰών {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.83; εὐ. κατὰ τάχος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.101}}; εὐ. παραχρῆμα (see. entry παραχρῆμα); εὐ. ἀπ᾽ αρχῆς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 84 (_anapaest meter_); εὐ. ἐξ ἀρχῆς 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.2.16}}; ἐξ ἀρχῆς εὐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1287b10; εὐ. κατ᾽ ἀρχάς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 24b; ἀφ᾽ ἑσπέρας εὐ. ἤδη 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Gall.” 1}}; εὐ. ἐκ νέου, ἐκ παιδός, **even** from one's youth, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 485d, 519a; εὐ. ἐκ παιδίου 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.20}}: with a _participle_, εὐ. νέοι ὄντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.39; εὐ. ἥκων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.7.2; εὐ. ἀπεκτονώς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.127}}; τοῦ θέρους εὐ. ἀρχομένου **just** at the beginning of summer, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.47}}; ἀρξάμενος εὐ. καθισταμένου [τοῦ πολέμου] from the **very** beginning of the war, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.1}}; εὐ. ἀποβεβηκότι **immediately** on disembarking, {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.43}}; εὐ. γενομένοις at **the moment of** birth, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 186b}}: _metaphorically_, **at once, naturally**, ὑπάρχει εὐθὺς γένη ἔχον τὸ ὄν Being falls **at once** into genera, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1004a5, compare “Po.” 1452a14}}: with _substantive_, ἡ τῶν Ἰταλιωτῶν εὐθὺς φυγή {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 8.1.5.}}
**B.II.2** less frequently in a local relation, ὑπὲρ τῆς πόλεως εὐ. **just** above the city, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.96}}; παρ᾽ αὐτὴν εὐ. ὁ ἔσπλους ἐστίν **directly** past it (the mole), {{Refs|[prev. author] 8.90}}; ἐγγύτατα τούτου εὐ. ἐχομένη **immediately** adjoining this, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. passage],compare 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 25.23}}; εὐ. ἐπὶ τὴν γέφυραν Foed. cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.118, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.2.1, 2, 2.4.24, “Ages.” 1.29}}; τὴν εὐ. Ἄργους κἀπιδαυρίας ὁδόν the road **leading straight to** Argos, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1197 (condemned by 9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc.); εὐ. Λυκείου 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 110, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 498a32, etc.}}
**B.II.3** of Manner, **directly, simply**, variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 100a.}}
**B.II.4** like{αὐτίκα} {{Refs|11:}}**for instance, to take the first example that occurs**, ὥσπερ ζῷον εὐθύς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1277a6, compare “Cael.” 284b10, etc.; οἷον εὐθύς 2nd c.AD: Cleomedes Astronomus 1.1, 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 11.145.}}
**C** regular _adverb_ **εὐθέως**, used just as εὐθύς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 31, “OC” 994, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 31, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 63a, etc.}}; αἰσθόμενος εὐθέως **as soon as** he perceived, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 3.11; ἐπεὶ εὐθέως ᾔσθοντο 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.2.4; εὐθέως παραχρῆμα 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.20, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 52.6.}}
**C.2** ={εὐθύς} {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 11.4, οἷον εὐθέως}} as **for example**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.52.1, 12.5.6 (uncertain meaning in 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 55)}}; so εὐ. alone, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.589. (εὐθέως}} is the commoner form in later Greek, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 34.17 (3rd c.BC), etc.)}}

2118:
** εὐθύτης** [ῠ], ητος, ἡ,
(εὐθύς) **straightness**, opposed to καμπυλότης, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Categoriae” 10a12}}; opposed to περιφέρεια, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Mete.” 385b30}}; εὐ. τριχῶν, opposed to οὐλότης, {{Refs|[prev. author] “GA” 782a3}}; ἡ εὐ. τῆς τάσεως the **direction**.., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.193.}}
**II** **righteousness**, {{LXX|LXX.Josh.24.14, +others}}

2119:
** εὐκαιρ-έω**
(late, _accusative_ to {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 103)}}, **have opportunity, leisure** or **time**, {{NT+3rd c.BC+|“PSI” 4.342.2 (3rd c.BC), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 20.9.4, NT.1Cor.16.12, etc.}}: with _infinitive_, οὐδὲ φαγεῖν ηὐκαίρουν {{NT+3rd c.BC+|NT.Mark.6.31, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.223d, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 33; τοῦ διαβῆναι “PEleph.” 29.7 (3rd c.BC).}}
**II** τινι or εἴς τι, **devote one's leisure** to a thing, εὐ. τοῖς ἀθανάτοις ἑαυτοῦ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Chio Epistolographus “Ep.” 16.6}}; εὐ. εἰς οὐδὲν ἕτερον ἢ.. {{NT|NT.Act.17.21.}}
**III** **enjoy good times, prosper**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.60.10; τοῖς βίοις [prev. author] 32.5.12: in this sense also εὐκαιρέομαι, 2nd-1st c.BC: Posidonius Historicus 59 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}
**III.2** **to be timely**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.64 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus (?) “Oxy.” 215ii2.}}

2120:
** εὐκαιρ-ία**,
_Ionic dialect_ -ιη, ἡ, **good season, opportunity**, τὴν εὐ. διαφυλάττειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.34}}; ἀπολαύειν τῆς εὐ. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 3 “fragment” 89}}; εὐκαιρίαν ζητεῖν ἵνα.. {{NT|NT.Matt.26.16}}; εὐ. τοῦ ἐλθεῖν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PMich.” in “Class.Phil.” 22.250 (2nd c.AD)}}; **leisure**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιστολαί” 17, 23; κατὰ πολλὴν εὐ. καὶ σχολήν 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 23}}—a usage condemned by [{{Refs 9th c.AD+|9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc. and Suid. see at {σχολή}.}}]
**II** **appropriateness**, opposed to ἀκαιρία, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 272a}}; μεταφορᾶς, στίχων, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.16b,736f.}}
**II.2** **convenient situation**, τῶν πόλεων {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 16.29.3, compare 4.44.11, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.4.}}
**II.3** **opportune supply**, ὑδάτων, of rainfall or irrigation, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 3.23.4 (_plural_), 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.52.}}
**III** **wealth, prosperity**, αἱ τῶν βίων εὐ. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.59.7, compare 13.9.2 (conjecture), etc.}}; εὐ. οὐκ ἔχει he has no **property**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“BGU” 665ii4 (1st c.AD).}}

2121:
** εὔκαιρ-ος**, ον,
**well-timed, seasonable**, λόγος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 113; θάνατος “Comica Adespota” 116}}; ὕδατα (rainfall) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 8.7.6}}; **nihil** -ότερον **epistula tua**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Atticum” 4.7.1}}: with _infinitive_, χὥ τι σοι λέγειν εὔκαιρόν ἐστι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 32, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 28.4; - ότερον ἔσται διελθεῖν 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 12.9.7}}; τὸ εὔκαιρον, ={εὐκαιρία}, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Dinarcho” 7; τὸ μέτριον καὶ τὸ εὔ. ἐν ἡδοναῖς Aristo Stoic. 1.86}}; εὔκαιρον ἀείσας **in season**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epigrammata - PLG” 5.5.}}
**II** of places, **convenient, well situated**, τόποι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 2p.28 (3rd c.BC, _superlative_),compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.38.1 (_superlative_), 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.63, etc.}}
**III** **rich, wealthy**, uncertain reading in St. Byzantine see at {Χαττηνία}.
**IV** _adverb_ -ρως **seasonably, opportunely**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἰητροῦ” 3, Nico 1, “PCair.Zen.” 38.28 (3rd c.BC), +others, etc.}}; εὐ. χρῆσθαί τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.143; εὐ. ἔχειν πρός τι [prev. author] 11.12, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 582a28}}; **favourably, propitiously,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Zenon Papyri du Caire 46 (3rd c.BC), “POxy.” 2086r. 6 (2nd c.AD, εὐκερως Papyrus)}}: _comparative_ -ότερον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 78a}}: _superlative_ -ότατα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.63.13.}}
**IV.2** οὐκ-ρως ἔχειν to have no **leisure**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.26.10.}}

2122:
Included with: ** εὔκαιρ-ος**, ον,
**well-timed, seasonable**, λόγος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 113; θάνατος “Comica Adespota” 116}}; ὕδατα (rainfall) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 8.7.6}}; **nihil** -ότερον **epistula tua**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Atticum” 4.7.1}}: with _infinitive_, χὥ τι σοι λέγειν εὔκαιρόν ἐστι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 32, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “De rerum natura - Wien. Stud.” 28.4; - ότερον ἔσται διελθεῖν 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 12.9.7}}; τὸ εὔκαιρον, ={εὐκαιρία}, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Dinarcho” 7; τὸ μέτριον καὶ τὸ εὔ. ἐν ἡδοναῖς Aristo Stoic. 1.86}}; εὔκαιρον ἀείσας **in season**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epigrammata - PLG” 5.5.}}
**II** of places, **convenient, well situated**, τόποι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 2p.28 (3rd c.BC, _superlative_),compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.38.1 (_superlative_), 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.63, etc.}}
**III** **rich, wealthy**, uncertain reading in St. Byzantine see at {Χαττηνία}.
**IV** _adverb_ -ρως **seasonably, opportunely**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἰητροῦ” 3, Nico 1, “PCair.Zen.” 38.28 (3rd c.BC), +others, etc.}}; εὐ. χρῆσθαί τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.143; εὐ. ἔχειν πρός τι [prev. author] 11.12, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 582a28}}; **favourably, propitiously,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Zenon Papyri du Caire 46 (3rd c.BC), “POxy.” 2086r. 6 (2nd c.AD, εὐκερως Papyrus)}}: _comparative_ -ότερον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 78a}}: _superlative_ -ότατα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.63.13.}}
**IV.2** οὐκ-ρως ἔχειν to have no **leisure**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.26.10.}}

2124:
** εὐλάβ-εια** [λᾰ], ἡ,
_Ionic dialect_ **-ιη**{{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 118, Agathoin “PLG” ii 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}:— **discretion, caution**, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus [same place], Agathol.c., etc.; ἐσῴζετ᾽ ἂν τὴν -ειαν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 994; personified in 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 782; ἡ εὐ. σῴζει πάντα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 376}}; εὐλάβειαν ἔχειν μή.., ={εὐλαβεῖσθαι μή}.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 321a}}; so εὐ. αὕτη.. τὸ μὴ νέους.. γεύεσθαι **caution to preuent** their tasting, {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 539b}}; εὐλαβείας οὐ μικρᾶς δεῖται, εὐ. ἐστὶ πολλῆς, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.262, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1269a14}}; εὐ. ποιητέον περί τινας{{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. work] 1315a17; δι᾽ εὐλαβείας ἔχειν ἀλλήλους 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 5.38}}; ἐπ᾽ εὐλαβεία.. προείρηται by way of **caution**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 539d}}; ἐπ᾽ εὐ. in [{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1024 is _falsa lectio_ for{ἐπ᾽ ἀβλ}., cf. Scholia (at [prev. work])}}]
**2** with _genitive_, **caution** or **discretion in** a thing, πολλὴ εὐ. τούτων ποιητέα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.3.11}}; εὐλάβειαι πληγῶν **auoidance of**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 815a; τῶν αἰσχρῶν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1121b24; ηὑλάβεια τῶν ποιουμένων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 116; εὐλάβειαν τῶνδε προυθέμην [prev. author] “El.” 1334}}; ἡ τῶν περιεχόντων εὐ. **careful employment of**.., {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 3 “fragment” 32.}}
**3** **reverence, piety**, πρὸς or περὶ τὸ θεῖον, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 13.12, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Numa” 22 (but also πρός τινα 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.581)}}: _absolutely_, **godly fear,** {{NT|NT.Heb.5.7, 12.28}}; **religious scruple**, οὐδεμίαν εὐ. προορωμένων {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 42.21 (2nd c.BC).}}
**3.b** astitle, ἡ σὴ εὐ. your **reverence,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|PFlor. 73.7 (6th c.AD).}}
**4** in bad sense, **over-caution, timidity**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Fabius Maximus” 1, 2.432e; εὐλαβίη γὰρ ἀπειρίη 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “ὀξέων νούσων θεραπευτικόν” 1.2.}}

2125:
** εὐλαβ-έομαι**,
_imperfect_ ηὐλαβούμην variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 748 (troch.), 1059, εὐλ- 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.25}}: _future_ -ήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 410a, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1127b6}}; also-ηθήσομαι {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|LXX.Deu.2.4, +others, 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 7.116, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 5.249:}}_aorist_ ηὐλαβήθην (or εὐλ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 89c}}:— **to be discreet, cautious, beware**, followed by μή or ὅπως μή with _subjunctive_, εὐ. μὴ φανῇς κακὸς γεγώς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1129, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 100, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 253, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 89c, etc.: by}}_future_ _indicative_, ὅπως μὴ.. οἰχήσομαι{{Refs|[prev. work] 91c (omitting codex B)}}: with _infinitive_, εὐλαβουμένῳ πεσεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 616; εὐ. λέγειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 101c: with μή}} inserted, εὐ. μὴ σῴζειν φίλους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1059, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1277, 5th c.BC(?): Cydias Lyricus 1.}}
**2** **take care**, ὅπως κατοίσεις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 955; εὐ. περί τι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 927c, “Ion” 537a; περί τινος [prev. author] “Lg.” 691b; ἀμφί τινι 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Gall.” 21: _absolutely_, εὐλαβήθηθ᾽ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 47; εὐλαβούμενος ἠρόμην 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 333e, compare 316d}}; εἰ μηδὲν εὐλαβηθέντα τἀληθὲς εἰπεῖν δέοι **without reserve**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.159}}; **take precautions**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1303b27.}}
**3** **incur risk**, εὐλαβήθη περὶ καθαιρέσεως δόξης Vett. Val.{{Refs|231.19, compare 209.11.}}
**II** with _accusative_, **have a care of, beware of**, εὐλαβοῦ στρόμβον.. μή σ᾽ ἀναρπάσῃ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 195}}; εὐ. τὴν κύνα **'ware** the dog, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1215; εὐ. πενίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 372c; τὸν φθόνον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.305; τὸ ψεῦδος 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1127b6; τοὺς ὑβρίζεσθαι νομίζοντας [prev. author] “Pol.” 1315a27; τὰς μυίας [prev. author] “HA” 611b11.}}
**II.2** **reverence, pay honour to**, τὸν θεόν {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 879e; τὸν δῆμον 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 7; in LXX}}, **fear** God, {{LXX+2nd c.AD+|LXX.Nah.1.7; εὐ. ἀπὸ τοῦ ὀνόματος Κυρίου LXX.Zeph.3.12; μηδὲν-ούμενον, ὡς οἱ πολλοὶ λέγουσιν, ὅτι.. δεῖ.. 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.49.}}
**II.3** **watch for, await quietly**, καιρόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 699.}}
**II.4** **put out of harm's way**, τὰ κοῖλα γαστρός {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 1411.}}
**III** later in _active_, εὐλαβεῖν τινα **beware of**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“BGU” 665.4 (1st c.AD)}}; compare εὐλάβησον, -ῆσαι, {{Refs 9th c.AD+|9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc.}}

2126:
** εὐλαβ-ής**, ές,
(λαβεῖν) **taking hold well, holding fast, clinging**, _metaphorically_, πενία {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 29}}: literal in _adverb_ εὐλαβῶς, κατέχειν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 3.13, 6.55 (_superlative_): but mostly}}
**II** _metaphorically_, **undertaking prudently, discreet, cautious**, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 91, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 311a, +others; τὸ εὐλαβές, ={εὐλάβεια}, [prev. work]b; εὐλαβὴς περί τι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “C. Gracchus” 3}}]; τὸ πρὸς τὰ μεγάλα τῶν τετολμημένων εὐ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 2.8.2}}; εὐ. ἀπό τινος **keeping** from.., {{LXX|LXX.Lev.15.31}}. _adverb_ - βῶς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 246b; εὐ. διακείμενος 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 13.12, etc.}}: _comparative_ -εστέρως {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1375; - έστερον διακεῖσθαι πρός τι 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.18.1.}}
**II.2** **reverent, pious**, [{{LXX+NT|LXX.Mic.7.2 (variant{εὐσεβής}), NT.Luke.2.25, NT.Act.2.5, etc.}}]: _superlative_ -έστατος, as title, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|Dionysius Epigrammaticus “Ep.” 71, 5th-6th c.AD: Procopius Gazaeus Rhetor “Epistulae - Epistolographi” 126.}}
**III** _passive_, **easy to get hold of**, κέρκος {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Lex.” 7.}}
**III.2** **cautiously undertaken** or **effected**, μετάβασις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 736d; ἡδοναί 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 15 ἀβλ-Reiske).}}

2127:
** εὐλογ-έω**,
_imperfect_ εὐλόγουν or ηὐλ- {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 454, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.206}}: _future_ -ήσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 465 (Lyric poetry):}}_aorist_ εὐλόγησα or ηὐλ{{LXX|- LXX.Gen.1.22, +others}}: _infinitive_ εὐλογῆσαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 565}}: _perfect_ εὐλόγηκα {{LXX|LXX.Num.23.11:—}}_passive_, with _future_ _middle_ εὐλογήσομαι (variant -ηθήσομαι, as always in {{LXX+5th c.BC+|LXX, LXX.2Ki.7.29, +others) 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.5}}: _aorist_ εὐλογήθην {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Phalaris Epistolographus “Epistulae - Epistolographi” 119.3 (_optative_)}}: _perfect_ εὐλόγημαι {{LXX|LXX.Ruth.2.19}}:—**speak well of, praise**, πόλιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 580; πατέρα τὸν ἀμόν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1314, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1.c., 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1.c., +others}}, Isoc.[same places]; **deliver a panegyric upon**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica ad Alexandrum” 1426a3}}: with _neuter_ _adjective_, εὐ. καὶ δίκαια κἄδικα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 372, compare “Ec.” 454}}; θεοὶ εὐλογοῦσί τινα **honour** him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 927}}:—_passive_, ἐπαίνοις εὐλογούμενον πέδον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 720; τὸν ἐν Δωδῶνι δαίμον᾽ εὐλογούμενον [prev. author] “fragment” 461.}}
**II** of God or men, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Gen.35.9, +others, NT.Act.3.26, +others: frequently in}}_perfect_ _participle_ _passive_ εὐλογημένος{{LXX+NT|as LXX.Deu.28.3, NT.Luke.1.28.}}
**II.2** **bless, praise** a god, {{Refs|“OGI” 73 (from Egypt), compare εὐ. τὴν Εἶσιν (sic) “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 4705c ([prev. passage])}}; σου τὰς δυνάμεις{{Refs|Buresch “Aus Lydien” 113}}; so in {{LXX+NT|LXX and NT, LXX.Josh.22.23, +others, NT.Jam.3.9.}}
**II.3** also, apparently by a Hebrew euphemism, **curse**, {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.20(21).10, cf. LXX.Job.2.5.}}

2129:
** εὐλογ-ία**, ἡ,
**good or fine language**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 400d, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Lex.” 1.}}
**2** **plausibility**, ἔχει τινὰ εὐλογίαν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 6.31.1}}; **habet** εὐλογίαν {{NT+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Atticum” 13.22.4, cf. NT.Rom.16.18}}; ἡ τοῦ δόγματος εὐ. probably in {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 27; ἡ εὐ. τῶν πραγμάτων [prev. author] “Herc.” 1251.8.}}
**II** **praise, eulogy**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 4.5, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.42; ὑμνῆσαι δι᾽ εὐλογίας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 356 (Lyric poetry); ἄξιος εὐλογίας 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 738: _plural_, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 3.3, 6(5).21, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 365a}}; **good fame, glory**, ἀγήραντος εὐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 100, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 5.24; εὐλογίαν φέρει “Lyrica Alexandrina Adespota” 21.10; ἔχειν εὐ. τινὰ πρός τινα “POxy.” 65.4 (3rd-4th c.AD).}}
**II.2** especially**praise** to God, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Sir.50.20(22), NT.Rev.7.12, “OGI” 74 (from Egypt).}}
**III** **act of blessing**, opposed to κατάρα, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Gen.27.12, NT.Jam.3.10.}}
**III.2** **blessing called down** or **bestowed**, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Pro.10.22, NT.1Pet.3.9.}}
**III.3** **gift, bounty**, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Josh.15.19, LXX.4Ki.5.15, NT.2Cor.9.5}}; ὁ σπείρων ἐπ᾽ εὐλογίαις ἐπ᾽ εὐλογίαις καὶ θερίσει **bountifully**,{{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. work] 6, compare 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.129.}}

2130:
** εὐμετά-δοτος**, ον,
**readily imparting, generous**, {{NT+4th c.BC+|NT.1Tim.6.18, Vett. Val. 46.24, +others, 5th c.AD: Hermias Alexandrinus Philosophus “in Platonis Phaedrum scholia” p.94 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus}}; τὸ εὐ. **generosity**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 1.14.}}
**II** _passive_, **easily imparted**, μυστήρια {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1014}}; of leprosy, **contagious**, {{Refs 7th c.AD+|7th c.AD: Paulus Aegineta Medicus 4.1}}. _adverb_ -τως [{{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus see at {εὐσυναλλάκτως}.}}]

2133:
** εὔνοιᾰ**, ἡ,
Ionic **εὐνοίη** (εὔνοιαν is _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.36)}}, _poetry_ **εὐνοΐη** {{Refs|“IG” 14.815}}: (εὔνους):— **goodwill, favour** (distinct from φιλία, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1155b33, 1166b30), κατὰ εὐνοίην 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.108; δι᾽ εὐνοίας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.40; δι᾽ εὔνοιαν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 337b}}; εὐνοίας ἕνεκα Docum. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.54, etc.; εὐνοίας ἕνεκα τῆς εἰς τὸν δῆμον “IG” 22.212.32, etc.}}; κατ᾽ εὔνοιαν κρίνειν **partially**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.4.1; κατ᾽ εὔνοιαν φρενῶν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 940; μετ᾽ εὐνοίας 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.9, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 241c, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.276, NT.Eph.6.7; ὑπ᾽ εὐνοίας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.9; εὐνοίῃ τι ποιῆσαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.239; εὐνοίᾳ λέγειν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1322; εὐνοίᾳ μᾶλλον ἢ ἐλέγχῳ τὰ γιγνόμενα δοκιμάζειν 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 31.22}}; εὐνοίᾳ τῇ σῇ **for the love** of you, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 486a: with objective _genitive_, ἐπ᾽ εὐνοίᾳ χθονός}} **for love** of fatherland, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 1012; εὐνοίᾳ τῇ ἑαυτοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 485a}}; ἕνεκα τῆς τῶν Ἑλλήνων εὐνοίας **goodwill towards** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.7.20}}; [εὔνοιαν] ἔχειν εἴς τινα Docum. cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.54; πρός τινα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 470a; πρὸς τὸν δῆμον “IPE” 12.32.7 (from Olbia), etc.}}; εὔ. παρὰ τῶν θεῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.1; εὔνοιαν ἔκ τινων κτᾶσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.2.22}}; εὔνοιαν παρασχεῖν to show **favour**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 708; ἔργῳ δεικνύναι 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.76}}; εὔνοιαν ἔχειν to **wish heartily** that.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.11; ὡς ἑκατέρων τις εὐνοίας.. ἔχοι [prev. author] 1.22; ἡ εὔ. παρὰ πολὺ ἐποίει τῶν ἀνθρώπων μᾶλλον ἐς τοὺς Λακεδαιμονίους [prev. author] 2.8}}: in _plural_, **impulses of kindness, favours**, τοῖς ἥσσοσιν γὰρ πᾶς τις εὐνοίας φέρει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 489; Ἀρτέμιδος εὐνοίαισι [prev. author] “Th.” 450; ταῖς εὐ. μεθ᾽ ὑμῶν ἦσαν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 14.15}}; but, **acts of kindness, favours**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 15.9.}}
**II** **gift** or **present in token of goodwill**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.282}}: _plural_, **benevolences**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. author] 8.25. [εὔνοια as dactyl, “Arch.Papyrus” 1.220 (twice, 2nd c.BC).]}}

2135:
** εὐνοῦχος**, ὁ,
(εὐνή, ἔχω) **castrated person, eunuch**, employed to take charge of the women and act as **chamberlain** (whence the name, ὁ τὴν εὐνὴν ἔχων), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.130, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 117, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.60, etc.}}
**2** of animals, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Heroicus” 1.3, Scholia Par. 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.585.}}
**3** of dates, **without stones**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 267:— 4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus name for θρίδαξ, Lycusap. 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 2.69e.}}
**II** as _adjective_, **watching the bed, sleepless**, λαμπάδες εὐνούχοισιν ὄμμασιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 789.}}

2136:
** εὐοδ-ία**, ἡ,
**a good journey**, καὶ σοὶ δ᾽ εὐοδίης τρίβον ὄλβιον εὔχομαι εἶναι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Arch.Papyrus” 1.221 (2nd c.BC): hence εὐοδίαν ἀπὸ στόματος χέειν}} **good wishes for one's success**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 36; personified, “OGI” 77 (Alexandria, 3rd c.BC)}}: _metaphorically_, κατ᾽ εὐοδίαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.27 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus: with _genitive_, εὐ. τοῦ ἐλθεῖν}} a **good opportunity** of coming, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PMich.” in “Class.Phil.” 22.250 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II** **success**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Demetrius Lacon Philosophus 1012.47 F., 1429.2 F.}}
**III** **permeability**, τῶν πόρων {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.86.}}

2137:
** εὐοδ-όω**,
**help on the way**, with _dative_ _person_, σφῷν δ᾽ εὐοδοίη Ζεύς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1435 (nisi to be read σφώ)}}: with _accusative_ _person_, {{LXX+4th c.BC+|LXX.Gen.24.27, “PSI” 4.299.14: _absolutely_, τὸ εὐοδοῦν 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 5.6.7.}}
**2** _passive_, **have a prosperous journey**, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Dan.8.11, +others, NT.Rom.1.10}}; of things, **prosper, be successful**, ὡς Κλεομένεϊ εὐωδώθη τὸ πρῆγμα see reading in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.73}}; θησαυρίζων ὅ τι ἂν εὐοδῶται, ={εὐπορῇ}, {{NT|NT.1Cor.16.2, cf. NT.Act.11.29.}}

2138:
** εὐπειθ-ής**, ές,
(compare εὐπιθής) **ready to obey, obedient**, τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 254a; τοῖς νόμοις -έστατος [prev. author] “Lg.” 715c, compare 890c: with _genitive_, τῶν νόμων [prev. work] 632b}}; -έστατοι πρὸς ἀρετήν{{Refs|[prev. work] 718c; εἴς τι [prev. author] “Phdr.” 271d}}; **compliant**, εὐ. γεγονέναι {{LXX+1st c.BC+|“BGU” 1104.23 (1st c.BC), LXX.4Mac.12.6}}. etc.; of things, as of the voice, **under control**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Audibilibus” 802a6}}; ὕλη (material) εἰς ἅπαν εὐ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.3}}; of food, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.669b.}} _adverb_ -θῶς{{Refs|[prev. work] 981a.}}
**II** _active_, **persuasive**, of a rein, ἐϋπειθέϊ δεσμῷ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 1.313; uncertain reading in 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus (see. εὐπιθής).}}

2141:
** εὐπορ-έω**,
_future_ -ήσω: _aorist_ εὐπόρησα: _perfect_ εὐπόρηκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 43.19, etc., ηὐπ- 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 297e, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.403f}}:—**prosper, thrive**, εὐποροῦσι γὰρ οἱ ὀλίγοι **are wealthy**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1280a4, compare “SIG” 344.116 (Teos, 4th c.BC)}}; εὐ. ἀπὸ τῶν πονηροτάτων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.7.4; οἱ εὐποροῦντες 4th c.BC: Amphis Comicus 15.6}}; of things, ὅθεν ὁ πόλεμος εὐπορεῖ from which sources war **is successfully maintained**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.34.}}
**b** with _genitive_ _of things_, **have plenty of, abound in**, χρημάτων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 19.25, 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 228.2; σίτων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.6.19}}; ῥημάτων, ὀνομάτων, λόγων, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Ion” 536c, “Sph.” 267d, “Smp.” 209b; ἐφοδίων 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Themistocles” 10}}; εὐ. ἵππων **gain possession of**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.1.10}}; εὐ. τῆς ἀληθείας **attain** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 996a16; also εὐ. ἔν τινι 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.66; τοῖς ἀναγκαίοις 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.17.2.}}
**2** **find a way, find means**, _absolutely_, ὡς ἕκαστοι ηὐπόρησαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.44, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 478a}}: with _infinitive_, **to be able** to do, ἐπιχειρεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 102a13 (also τοῦ πολλὰ λέγειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 235a)}}; also εὐπορῶ ὅ τι λέγω I **have plenty** to say, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ion” 532c}}: with _participle_, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 634b}}; τοῦτο εὐ. **to be provided with an answer** on this point, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Euthd.” 279a}}; οὐκ εὐ. ὅπῃ.. not **to know how** to do, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Smp.” 219e; μᾶλλον εὐ. πρὸς τὴν γνῶσιν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 644b28.}}
**II** c._accusative_ _of things_, **supply** or **furnish**, τἀργύριον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 7.8; δέκα μνᾶς τινι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 33.7}}; **procure**, ἄλλοθεν χρήματα {{Refs|[prev. author] 40.36; ὅθεν σιτοπομπίας εὐπόρησε τοῖς στρατιώταις [prev. author] 23.155}}; **bring forward**, ἀποδείξεις {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 2.31}}; **find available**, μὴ -ήσας πλοῖον (to be read πλοίων) {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1068.3 (3rd c.AD)}}:—hence in _passive_, = _intransitive_ _active_, **have plenty of, abound in**, τινος {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Oeconomica” 1347b4; μαθητῶν NT.Act.11.29}}; τινι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.43.8}}; **obtain the use of**, πλοίου {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PFlor.” 367.8 (3rd c.AD): _absolutely_, οἱ εὐπορούμενοι “SIG” 495.66 (Olbia, 3rd c.BC),compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Bis Acc.” 27, “PMag.Par.” 1.3125:}} — εὐπορηθέν in strict _passive_ sense, **being furnished**, Ps.-{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Vitae Homeri” 210.}}
**III** as Philos. term, opposed to ἀπορέω, **have one's doubts resolved, gain clear knowledge**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 80c, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 995a27; εὐ. περί τινος [prev. author] “de An.” 403b21.}}

2142:
** εὐπορ-ία**, ἡ,
(εὔπορος) **ease, facility**, of doing a thing, with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 100.5; ναῦς εὐ. ἦν ποιεῖσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.52: _absolutely_, ὅτε πολλὴ ὑμῖν εὐ. φαίνεται 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.6.37}}: with _genitive_ _of things_, **easy means of providing**, τοῦ βίου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 321e; τοῦ καθ᾽ ἡμέραν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.82}}; also εὐ. ἐν τῇ τέχνῃ, ἐκ τῆς τέχνης, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 24.5; εὐ. τῆς τύχης 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.45}}; εὐπορίαν τῇ βδελυρίᾳ τῇ ἑαυτοῦ τοὺς συμμάχους ποιεῖσθαι to make them **a means of satisfying** his brutal passions, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.107}}; ἡ παρ᾽ ἀλλήλων εὐ. mutual **assistance**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.67.}}
**2** **plenty, abundance**, opposed to πενίη, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 101; χρημάτων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.8.28; ἀγαθῶν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1091b26}}; ἡ περὶ τὸν βίον εὐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.7}}; ἡ περὶ τὴν οὐσίαν εὐ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1326b34}}: _absolutely_, **welfare**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.7}}; opposed to ἀπορία, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1279b27}}: in _plural_, **advantages**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.253, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 5.8; εὐπορίαι προσόδων 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1293a3}}; ἀρουραίη εὐ. rustic **wealth,** {{Refs|Anthologia Graeca 9.373.6}}; μιῆς ὄϊος καὶ βοὸς εὐ. **consisting of** one sheep or ox, {{Refs|[prev. work]149 (Antip.)}}; ἡ Εὐ. θεά {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“SIG” 1111 (Piraeus, 3rd c.AD).}}
**II** opposed to ἀπορία, **solution of doubts** or **difficulties**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 15c}}; opposed to ἀμηχανία, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 9.1; ἡ ὕστερον εὐ. λύσις τῶν πρότερον ἀπορουμένων 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 995a29}}; **resourcefulness**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “κατ᾽ ἰητρεῖον” 7.}}

2143:
** εὐπρέπ-εια**, ἡ,
**goodly appearance, comeliness**, εὐπρεπείᾳ προέχειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.31}}; opposed to ἀπρέπεια, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 274b, +others}}; **majesty**, εὐ. τῆς δόξης {{LXX+NT|LXX.Jer.23.9, cf. NT.Jam.1.11}}; **dignity**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“SIG” 880.19 (Pizus, 3rd c.AD)}}; ἐστεφάνωσε ἁ πόλις.. -είας καὶ εὐνοίας ἕνεκα τᾶς ἐς τὰν πόλιν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 4.1418 (Epid., 4th c.BC).}}
**II** **speciousness, plausibility**, εὐπρεπείᾳ λόγου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.11, 82; ἔχει.. εὐπρέπειαν μᾶλλον ἢ ἀλήθειαν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 305e}}; **pretext**, with _infinitive_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pyrrhus” 23.}}

2147:
** εὑρίσκω**,
_imperfect_ ηὕρισκον or εὕρ- {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 68, etc.}}: _future_ εὑρήσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Mercurium” 302, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.67, etc.}}: _aorist 2_ εὗρον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.498, etc., later ηὗρον or εὗρον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 553, etc.}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ εὕροσαν {{LXX+1st c.AD+|LXX.Deu.31.17, “BGU” 1201.16 (1st c.AD); _imperative_ εὑρέ Hdn.Gr. 2.23}}; _Epic dialect_ _infinitive_ εὑρέμεναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.393}}: later _aorist 1_ εὕρησα {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Manetho Astrologus 5.137}}; εὗρα variant in {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Luke.8.35, NT.Act.5.10, (ἐν-) “PGen.” 3.19 (2nd c.AD)}}: _perfect_ εὕρηκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 546, etc.}}, _perfect_ _imperative_ _2nd pers._ _singular_ εὕρηκε {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Nausicrates Comicus 1 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}:—_middle_, _future_ εὑρήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.6, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.9, etc.}}: _aorist 2_ εὑρόμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, _Attic dialect_ ηὑρ- or εὑρ- 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 269, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.58, etc.}}: _aorist 1_ εὑράμην {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Fragmenta” 116.3 (testes omnes), 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 12.34.4, 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de vita Pythagorica” 35.255, “Anthologia Graeca” 9.29 (1st c.AD: Antiphilus Epigrammaticus), epigram cited in 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 6.20.14, NT.Heb.9.12, “IG” 3.900 (2nd c.AD)}}:—_passive_, _future_ εὑρεθήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 108, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1105, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.41}}: _aorist 1_ ηὑρέθην or εὑρέθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1135}}, etc.: _perfect_ ηὕρημαι or εὕρ- {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 743 (troch), etc.— 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} has only _aorist_ _active_ and _middle_, except ἔθ᾽ εὑρίσκω (variant{ἐφευρίσκω}) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.158. (Earlier _Attic dialect_ Inscrr. have ηὑρέθην, ηὕρημαι, as “IG” 22.1636.32, +others, “epigram Gr.” 35 (4th c.BC): εὑρέθην “SIG” 679.80 (5th c.BC: Magnes Comicus Mae., 2nd c.BC)}}: the augment is seldom found in Papyri, ηὕρισκεν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 3p.101 (3rd c.BC)}}; never in those of {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus or 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus)}}:—**find**, εὗρεν δ᾽ εὐρύοπα Κρονίδην ἄτερ ἥμενον ἄλλων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.498, etc.}}; εὕρημα εὑ., see at {εὕρημα}.
**2** with _participle_, **find that**.., εὕρισκε Λακεδαιμονίους.. προέχοντας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.56, compare 1.5}}:—and in _passive_, ἤν εὑρεθῇς μὴ δίκαιος ὤν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 411, compare “OT” 839, “OC” 946: with}}_participle_ omitted, ὅταν τοὺς θεοὺς εὕρω κακούς (i.e. ὄντας) {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 452}}; εὑρήσει τοσαῦτα ἔτη (i.e. ὄντα) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.26}}; θῆλυς εὕρημαι (i.e. ὤν) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1075; ἄνους ηὑρέθη [prev. author] “Aj.” 763.}}
**3** with _infinitive_, εὕρισκε πρῆγμά οἱ εἶναι.. **found** that the thing for him was.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.79:}} —_middle_, εὑρίσκεται (sed to be read εὕρισκέ τε) ταῦτα καιριώτατα εἶναι{{Refs|[prev. work] 125}}:—_active_, also, **find means, be able**, οὐχ εὑρίσκει χρήσασθαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.12.2.}}
**4** εὑ. ὅπως.. **to find** by what means.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.67}}:—_middle_, c._infinitive_, **find out** or **discover how to**.., ηὕρετο.. παύειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 196 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**5** _passive_, εὑρέθη ὅτι.. **it was found that**.., {{LXX|LXX.1Esd.2.22(26).}}
**6** **befall**, of evils, τινα{{LXX|LXX.Gen.44.34, LXX.Deu.31.17.}}
**II** **find out, discover**, οὐδέ τι μῆχος εὑρέμεναι δυνάμεσθα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.393; οὐδέ τι τέκμωρ εὑρέμεναι δύνασαι 4.374, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.31; εὑ. ὁδόν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 10.29; ἐξ ἀμηχάνων πόρον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 59; μηχανὴν σωτηρίας [prev. author] “Th.” 209; πημάτων ἄρηξιν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 875; τινα ἐμοῦ βελτίονα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 104, etc.: _absolutely_, εὕρηκα 3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra cited in 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1094c}}:—_middle_, εὕρετο τέκμωρ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.472}}; ὄνομ᾽ εὕρεο **think of** a name to give him, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.403; εἴ τιν᾽ ἑταίροισιν θανάτου λύσιν.. εὑροίμην 9.422.}}
**II.2** with _infinitive_, **get a chance of, be able**, ἵνα εὕρωμεν ἐπιστολὴν γράψαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 822.28 (2nd-3rd c.AD), compare 17, 20, “PGrenf.” 1.64.3 (6th c.AD), etc.}}
**III** **devise, invent**, ὀχήματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 468, etc.; πρόφασιν 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.65:}} —_middle_, τὰ δ᾽ ἔργα τοὺς λόγους εὑρίσκεται deeds **make themselves** words, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 625.}}
**IV** **get, gain**, ἀρετάν, δόξαν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.89, “P.” 2.64; τὰ χρήματ᾽ ἀνθρώποισιν εὑρίσκει φίλους 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 88; ἐξ ὀλβίων ἄζηλον εὑροῦσαι βίον [prev. author] “Tr.” 284, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1107 (_anapaest meter_); δεινὰ δ᾽ εὑροῦσαν πρὸς αὐθαίμων πάθη 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1078 (Lyric poetry); ἀφ᾽ ὧν ὄνασιν εὕρωσι [prev. author] “El.” 1061}}; μέγ᾽ εὑρεῖν κέρδος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1305; εὑ. σωτηρίαν τῷ ἀνθρώπῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 321c}}; εὑ. μητρὶ φόνον **bring about** murder, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 650}}: _absolutely_, **acquire wealth**, {{LXX|LXX.Lev.25.47}}:—_middle_, **find** or **get for oneself, bring on oneself**, οἷ.. αὐτῷ πρώτῳ κακὸν εὕρετο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.304}} (so in _active_, μή πού τις ἐπίσπαστον κακὸν εὕρῃ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|24.462); αὐτὸς ηὑρόμην πόνους 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 269; μοῖραν ηὕρετ᾽ ἀσφαλῆ [prev. author] “Ag.” 1588, compare “Th.” 880 (Lyric poetry)}}: so in _perfect_ _passive_, μέγα πένθος ηὕρηται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 615 (Lyric poetry)}}; εὑρήσεται τιμωρίην **will get for himself, obtain**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.148, compare 9.26; ἀλεωρήν [prev. author] 9.6; κλέος 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.111; ἄδειαν εὑρόμενος 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.15; ἀτέλειαν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.1; εὑρίσκεσθαι ὠφελίαν ἀπό τινος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.31; τι παρά τινος “IG” 12.108.47, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.9}}; εὑ. παρά τινος with _infinitive_, **procure** from him that.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.28; δεηθέντες οὐκ ἐδύναντο εὑρέσθαι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 14.20.}}
**V** especially of merchandise, etc., **fetch, earn** money, εὑροῦσα πολλὸν χρυσίον **having fetched** a large sum, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.196; ηὗρε πλέον ἢ ἐνενήκοντα τάλαντα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.4.24, compare “Vect.” 4.40}}; οἰκία εὑρίσκουσα δισχιλίας (i.e. δραχμάς) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 8.35}}; ἐγδίδομεν.. τοὺς θριγκοὺς.. ὅτι ἂν εὕρωσιν for what they **will fetch,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 7.3073.7 (Lebad.)}}; ἐρωτᾶν τί εὑρίσκει what it **will fetch**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 15.4.}}
**V.2** of the sum or bid which **secures** an article or contract, οἰκέτην.. ἀποδίδοται τοῦ εὑρόντος sells **for what he will fetch**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.5.5; τοῦ ἤδη εὑρίσκοντος ἀπεδίδοτο 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.96, compare “SIG” 966.37 (Attica, 4th c.BC), 581.99 (Rhodes-Hierapytna, 2nd c.BC)}}; ἐκτιθέτωσαν τὸ εὑρίσκον ἐφ᾽ ἡμέρας δέκα the **highest** or **winning** bid, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PRev.Laws” 48.16 (3rd c.BC),compare “UPZ” 112vi9 (3rd c.BC)}}; προσέβαλον αὐτῷ τοῦ εὑρίσκοντος ἀνὰ [x] ἱερεῖα [x] I have placed at his disposal [x] pigs at the **current price** of [x], {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 161.5 (3rd c.BC),compare “UPZ” 114(1).24 (2nd c.BC).}}

2150:
** εὐσέβεια**, ἡ
(compare εὐσεβία), **reverence towards the gods** or **parents, piety** or **filial respect**, εὐ. εἰς θεοὺς καὶ γονέας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 615c, etc.; μιαίνων εὐσέβειαν Ἄρης 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 344 (Lyric poetry)}}; εὐ. Ζηνός **towards** him, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1097 (Lyric poetry); πρὸς εὐσέβειαν, ={εὐσεβῶς}, [prev. work] 464}}]; εὐ. πρός, περὶ τοὺς θεούς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 193d, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.124, compare 10.58; πρὸς ἀδελφόν 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 48.5}}; ἡ πρὸς τὸ θεῖον εὐ. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Inscription Prien.” 117.63 (1st c.BC), etc.}}; τὴν εὐ. τῶν πραχθέντων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.2.12}}: _plural_, **acts of piety**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica ad Alexandrum” 1423b28.}}
**2** **loyalty**, ἡ ὑμετέρα πρός με εὐ. {{Refs|“PLond.” 3.1178.14 (from Claudius)}}; ἡ εἴς με εὔνοια καὶ εὐ. {{Refs|“SIG” 814.2 (from Nero).}}
**3** = Latin {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Pietas”, 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 2.104, Mitteis “Chr.” 71.12 (V 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus), 5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Fragmenta” 159, etc.}}
**4** **credit** or **character for piety**, εὐσέβειαν οἴσῃ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 968.}}

2152:
** εὐσεβ-ής**, ές
(_dative_ _plural_ - σεβέοις {{Refs|“IG” 5(1).1390.5 (from Andania))}}, (σέβω) **pious, religious**, opposed to δυσσεβής (which see), {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1141, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.141, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 3.41; τρόπος 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 457 (Lyric poetry)}}: not common in early Prose, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Gorgias Rhetor et Sophista “fragment” 6D., 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 39e}}; **dutiful**, especially **discharging sacred duties**, πρός or ἔς τινα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 340, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 253}}; ἀνὴρ εὐ. (variant{εὐλαβής}) κατὰ τὸν νόμον {{NT|NT.Act.22.12; εὐ. καὶ φοβούμενος τὸν θεόν [prev. work] 10.2: with _accusative_ modi, -εστέρα χεῖρα}} **more righteous** in act, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 141; εὐσεβεῖς κἀξ εὐσεβῶν βλαστόντας 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 589}}; ὁ τῶν εὐσεβῶν χῶρος, of a place in the nether world, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 371c}}; ἐν εὐσεβέων (i.e. χώρῳ) {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 12, compare “Sammelb.” 2048 (ii b.c.).}}
**b** Astrology texts, αἱ τῶν εὐσεβῶν μοῖραι {{Refs|“Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 8(4).227.}}
**2** as epithet of Emperors, = **Pius,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|Inscriptiones Graecae ad res Romanas pertinentes 3.91 (3rd c.AD), +others, “PGrenf.” 1.49.28 (3rd c.AD), “PHamb.” 1.13.2 (3rd c.AD), etc.}}; especially of Antoninus Pius, {{Refs|“IGRom.” 3.1293, +others}}
**2.b** of taxes, etc., due to the Emperor, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“BGU” 917.15 (4th c.AD), etc.}}
**3** _metaphorically_, of a piece of land, **dutiful**, i.e. **productive**, ἀγρὸν -έστερον γεωργεῖν οὐδ᾽ ἕνα οἶμαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Γεωργός” 35.}}
**II** ofacts, things, etc., **holy, sacred**, ταῦτά μοὐστὶν εὐσεβῆ θεῶν πάρα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 122; εὐ. χρηστηριον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 1272}}; ἐν εὐσεβεῖ [ἐστι] c._infinitive_, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Hel.” 1277}}; τὸ εὐ., ={εὐσέβεια}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1125, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 43}}; τὸ ὑμέτερον εὐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.96}}; τοὐμὸν εὐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 656}}; τιτῶν ἐν ἀνθρώποις εὐσεβῶν παραβαίνειν Philipp. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.157.}}
**III** _adverb_ εὐσεβέως, _Attic dialect_ -βῶς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.79, etc.}}; εὐσεβῶς ἔχει, for εὐσεβές ἐστι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1431, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.212}}: _comparative_ -έστερον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.3.16}}: _superlative_ -έστατα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.33.}}

2153:
