2154:
** εὔσημ-ος**,
_Doric dialect_ **εὔσᾱμος**, ον, **of good signs** or **omens**, φάσμα ναυβάταις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 252 (Lyric poetry), compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Caesar” 43; ἱερά 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 8.7.12; [πῦρ] [prev. work] 1.31.}}
**II** **easily known by signs, conspicuous**, εὔσημον γὰρ οὔ μελανθάνει [τὸ πλοῖον] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 714; καπνῷ δ᾽ ἁλοῦσα.. εὔ. πόλις [prev. author] “Ag.” 818; σημεῖα 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Mochl.” 16; τόπος “Annuario” 4 /5.225 (Rhodes, 2nd c.BC)}}; ἴχνη {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 6.19.5 (_comparative_)}}; οὐκ εὔσημον, ὅθεν.. not **easy to distinguish**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 3.8.2}}; **legible, clear**, ὅπως -σαμοτέρα ὑπάρχῃ ἁ ἀναγραφά {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 1023.96 (Cos, 3rd-2nd c.BC)}}; εὔ. γράμματα {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“OGI” 665.12 (Egypt, 1st c.AD)}}; εὔ. προσαγόρευσις {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 381}}; of sound, **distinct**, βοαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1021; ἦχοι 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ ποιημάτων” 2.16 (_superlative_)}}; **wellmarked**, βραχίων {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “de gymnastica” 35; οὐλὴ εὔσημος “PPetr.” 1p.54 (3rd c.BC).}}
**II.2** **clear, intelligible**, λόγον εὔ. δοῦναι {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.1Cor.14.9; διδασκαλία 1st c.AD: Erotianus Grammaticus “Prooem.”, 1st-2nd c.AD: Heliodorus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 48.20.7.}}
**II.3** **evident**, τισι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.91 W., compare 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Abstinentia - Porphyrii Opuscula” 3.5.}}
**II.4** εὔσημα, τά, = Latin _insignia_, _falsa lectio_ for{σύσσημα}, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 36.2.}}
**II.5** of garments, **with fine edging**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 1564.11 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II** _adverb_ -μως **clearly, distinctly**, ἔχειν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 363a27; μεμνῆσθαι 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 10.2.23; προσανένεγκε “POxy.” 1188.5 (1st c.AD)}}: _superlative_ -ότατα {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1022a.}}

2155:
** εὔσπλαγχν-ος**, ον,
**with healthy bowels**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προρρητικόν” 2.6.}}
**II** **compassionate**, {{LXX+NT+6th c.AD+|LXX.PrMan.7, NT.Eph.4.32, NT.1Pet.3.8, “PMag. Leid.V.” 9.3, “PMasp.” 20.11 (6th c.AD).}}

2157:
** εὐσχημ-οσύνη**, ἡ,
**gracefulness, elegance**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 196a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.1.5}}; **decorum**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1128a25}}; **refinement**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pol.” 1329b28}}; βίου, ῥημάτων, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 588a, “Lg.” 627d (but also κίβδηλος εὐ}}. a spurious **respectability**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 366b).}}
**2** of the body, {{NT|NT.1Cor.12.23}}; ἡ τοῦ σώματος εὐ. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“IGRom.” 4.1029.35 (Astypalaea, 1st c.BC).}}
**II** **proper treatment, adequate maintenance,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 9(1).189 (from Tithora).}}

2158:
** εὐσχήμ-ων**, ον,
_genitive_ ονος, (σχῆμα) **elegant in figure, mien and bearing, graceful**, opposed to ἀσχήμων, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 413e, +others; ἀλεκτρυών 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 108}}; τὰ εὐ. ἡμῶν (i.e. μόρια) {{NT|NT.1Cor.12.24}}: _comparative_ -έστερος **more respectable**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 554e}}: _superlative_ -έστατοι, πονεῖν ἵπποι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “de Equitandi ratione” 11.12.}}
**2** in bad sense, **with an outside show of goodness, specious in behaviour**, εἴς τινα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 584.}}
**II** of things, **decent, becoming**, λόγοι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Hipp.” 490, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 60.9; πρᾶγμα οὐδαμῶς εὔσχημον λέγειν 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.162; λέγειν εὐσχήμονα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1128a7}}; τὸ εὔσχημον **decorum**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 401c, “Lg.” 797b.}} _adverb_ -μόνως **with grace and dignity, like a gentleman**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1210, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.8, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1101a1; ζῆν 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Herc.” 1251.18}}: Comp -έστερον, ἔχειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epinomis” 981a; τι φέρειν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 60.35}}: _superlative_ -έστατα {{Refs|“IG” 22.1034.11.}}
**II.2** later also, **noble, honourable**, in rank (condemned by {{NT+1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 309), NT.Mark.15.43, NT.Act.13.50, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Vita” 9, 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 66.7, +others}}; ἡ εὐ. **the noble lady,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|PFlor. 16.20 (3rd c.AD).}}
**II.2.b** title of a village magistrate, in _plural_, εὐ. κώμης {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 147 (2nd-3rd c.AD): _sing._, ἡ οἰκία τοῦ εὐ. “PRyl.” 236.15 (3rd c.AD).}}

2159:
Included with: ** εὔτον-ος**, ον,
(τείνω) **well-strung, vigorous**, of men's bodies or limbs, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 3.17, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Incessu Animalium” 710a31, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Anach.” 24, “Anthologia Graeca” 12.216 (2nd c.AD: Strato Epigrammaticus)}}: _comparative_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 693}}; of men, - ώτεροι τοῖς σώμασι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.3}}; τὸ εὔ., ={εὐτονία}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 815a, etc.: especially in Stoic philosophical (compare τόνος), 3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 3.121, 123}}; of engines, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 8.5.2 (_comparative_)}}; of the wind, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.41}}; of wine, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mirabilia” 832a11}}; τὸ εὔ., name of an eyesalve, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 7.115; εὔ. πληγή 2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “Belopoeica” 74.12.}}
**b** **distended**, εὐτόνῳ φλεβί (obscene meaning) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Neophron Tragicus (?) “Medea” in “PLit.Lond.” 77 “fragment” 2.7.}}
**c** **elastic, yielding**, -ώτερος χαλκοῦ χρυσός Porph.ad {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.259.}}
**2** **active, energetic**, πρόνοια {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1468.7 (3rd c.AD)}}; προσοχή {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “Protrepticus” 21.κά (_superlative_)}}; of persons, -ώτατος εἶναι with _participle_, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“OGI” 315.52 (Pessinus, 2nd c.BC).}}
**3** of an orator, **forcible**, εὔ. τῇ φράσει {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Veterum Censura” 5.4; τῆς λέξεως τὸ εὔ. [prev. work] 3.2, cf. Ph [prev. author] “Po.” 5.5.}}
**4** _adverb_ -νως **with might and main, vigorously**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1095, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 9.6, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 885a6, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.311, NT.Luke.23.10}}: _comparative_ -ώτερον, τοῦ δέοντος ἀφιᾶσι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nigr.” 36.}}
**5** **strenuously**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Herc.” 1251.23, “Po.” 2p.274H.}}
**6** **peremptorily**, -ώτερον ἐπιστεῖλαι, γράψαι, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PLille” 1.3i14 (3rd c.BC), “PPetr.” 2p.22, 3p.132 (compare p. x) (3rd c.BC).}}
**II** of the voice, **well-pitched**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 786b8. (Sts. as variant in codices for ἔντονος, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus [same place]}}; εὔτονος is perhaps _falsa lectio_ for{ἔντονον} in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 966.)}}

2160:
** εὐτρᾰπελ-ία**, ἡ,
**ready wit, liveliness**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ εὐσχημοσύνης” 7, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 563a, 3rd c.BC: Posidippus Comicus 28.5, 1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Familiares” 7.32.1, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 15.6}}: _plural_, **pleasantries**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 177; defined by 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus as πεπαιδευμένη ὕβρις, “Rh.” 1389b11, compare “EN” 1108a24}}; ἡ περὶ τὰς παιδιὰς καὶ τὰς ὁμιλίας εὐ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Antonius” 43.}}
**2** rarely in bad sense, ={βωμολοχία}, {{NT|NT.Eph.5.4.}}

2162:
** εὐφημ-ία**, ἡ,
**use of words of good omen**, opposed to δυσφημία:
**I** **abstinence from inauspicious language, religious silence**, εὐφημίαν ἴσχε, ={εὐφήμει}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 178; εὐ. ἐσχηκέναι πρός τινα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 717c}}; εὐφημία ʼστω, εὐφημία ʼστω, as a proclamation of **silence** before a prayer, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 295, compare “Av.” 959; so εὐφημίαν.. κηρύξας ἔχω 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 893; Ταλθύβιος.. εὐφημίαν ἀνεῖπε καὶ σιγὴν στρατῷ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1564; μετ᾽ εὐ. διδάσκειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 949b; ἐν εὐ. χρὴ τελευτᾶν [prev. author] “Phd.” 117e; πρὸς εὐφημίαν τρεπέσθω 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Laps.” 17.}}
**II** in positive sense, **auspiciousness**, λόγων εὐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 608, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.169; πᾶσαν εὐ. παρειχόμην 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Epistulae” 2.19}}; especially **a fair** or **honourable name** for a bad thing, **euphemism** (as Εὐμενίδες, εὐφρόνη, etc.), δι᾽ εὐφημίας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 736a; εὐφημίας ἕνεκα 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.92, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.449a.}}
**II.2** _falsa lectio_ for{εὐφωνία}, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 175.}}
**III** **prayer and praise, worship**, offered to the gods, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1469}}; ={εὐχή}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus2.149b; εὐξάμενον μετ᾽ εὐφημίας 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 2.14: _plural_, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 10.35.}}
**III.2** **honour, good repute** enjoyed by men, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ind.Sto.” 16,20}}; ἀθάνατος εὐ. D.S1.2; opposed to δυσφημία, {{NT|NT.2Cor.6.8}}; ἀδιάλειπτος {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.121e}}; ἡ ὕστερον εὐ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 31.20}}; τὴν παρὰ πᾶσιν ἀγαθὴν εὐ.good **repute,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 12(5).860.39 (Tenos, 1st c.BC)}}; ἡ ἐκ τῶν ξένων εὐ. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“OGI” 339.30 (Sestos, 2nd c.BC)}}; **panegyric**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 3.106a, 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 62.3}}; ἡ εὐ. σου, as a form of address, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“PLond.” 3.891.9 (4th c.AD)}}; αἱ εὐ. **plaudits, acclamations** in a local senate, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“POxy.” 2110.2 (4th c.AD).}}

2163:
** εὔφημ-ος**,
_Doric dialect_ **εὔφᾱμος**, ον, (φήμη) **uttering sounds of good omen**, ἀετός {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 618b31: usually in derived senses}}
**I** **abstaining from inauspicious words**, i.e. **religiously silent**, εὔφημον.. κοίμησον στόμα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1247; γλῶσσαν εὔ. φέρειν [prev. author] “Ch.” 581; so perhaps εὔ. γόοι [prev. author] “fragment” 40}}; εὐφάμου στόμα φροντίδος ἱέντες moving the lips of **reverent** thought, i.e. keeping a holy silence, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 132 (Lyric poetry)}}; so ὑπ᾽ εὐφήμου βοῆς, i.e. in silence, {{Refs|[prev. author] “El.” 630}}; εὔφημα φώνει, ={εὐφήμει}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Aj.” 362, 591, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 687; εὔφημος ἴσθι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 478; εὔ. πᾶς ἔστω λαός 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 39 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**I.2** **mild, softening** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare (εὐφημία 11.1, εὐφημισμός), ἐν-οτάτοις ὀνόμασι.. κατονομάζειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus2.140c}}; πρὸς τὸ -ότατον, Latin _in meliorem partem_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Prom.Es” 3}}. _adverb_ _comparative_ -ότερον {{Refs|12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1398.49.}}
**I.3** **fair-spoken**, εἰς τὸ δαιμόνιον {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Pietate - Herkulanische Studien” 18.}}
**II** in positive sense, **fair-sounding, auspicious**, μῦθοι {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 1.14; ἦμαρ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 636; ἔπος [prev. author] “Supp.” 512; εὔφημοι κέλαδοι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1072 (Lyric poetry); εὔφαμον δ᾽ ἐπὶ βωμοῖς μοῦσαν θείατ᾽ ἀοιδοί 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 694 (Lyric poetry); Μούσης ἀνοίγειν.. εὔφημον στόμα 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1719}}; εὔ. πόνοι **pious, holy**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 134 (Lyric poetry); δόμοι [prev. author] “Andr.” 1144}}; ᾠδῆς γένος, ἐρωτήματα, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 800e, “Hp.Ma.” 293a, cf. NT.Phil.4.8; πλοῦς 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de vita Pythagorica” 3.16 (_superlative_)}}. _adverb_ -μως **with** or **in words of good omen,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Hymnus ad Apollinem 171 (uncertain reading), 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 287, “IG” 12.108.55, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 265c}}: _comparative_ -ότερον {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Aristaenetus Rhetor 2.9.}}
**III** **laudatory**, λόγοι εὔ. **panegyrics**, [{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 31.3.4. (Also _falsa lectio_ for{εὔχυμος} 6th c.AD: Aëtius Medicus 5.58, for εὔφιμος 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Alexipharmaca” 275.)}}]

2164:
** εὐφορ-έω**,
**bear well, be productive**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιστολαί” 10, NT.Luke.12.16, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.64, +others: _metaphorically_, λόγος εὐ. πλημμύραις ῥημάτων καὶ ὀνομάτων [prev. author] 1.690 (ἐμφ- codex)}}: with _accusative_, εὐ. σταφυλάς {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 1.547.}}
**II** of ships, **have a prosperous voyage**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Lex.” 15.}}

2165:
** εὐφραίνω**,
_Epic dialect_ ἐϋφρ-, _future_ _Attic dialect_ εὐφρᾰνῶ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 742, etc.}}, _Ionic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ εὐφρανέω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.688, ἐϋφρανέω 7.297:}}_aorist 1_ εὔφρᾱνα or ηὔφρ-{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 155.12, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 7(6).3, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 217, etc.}}; _Epic dialect_ εὔφρηνα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.102}}, _subjunctive_ ἐϋφρήνῃς {{Refs|7.294}}:—_passive_, with _future_ _middle_ εὐφρᾰνοῦμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 7.5}}; _Ionic dialect_ _2nd pers._ _singular_ εὐφρανέαι (variant -έεαι) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.9}}; also _passive_ εὐφρανθήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 165, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.191, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 68:}}_aorist 1_ εὐφράνθην or ηὐ- {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.62, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 5}}: (εὔφρων):—**cheer, gladden**, εὐφρανέειν ἄλοχον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.688; ἐϋφραίνοιτε γυναῖκας 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.44; ἀνδρὸς ἐϋφραίνοιμι νόημα 20.82; εὐ. θυμόν τινος 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 7(6).3}}; νόον, φρένα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 742, “Supp.” 515; [τινὰ] ἐπέεσσι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.102; τινὰ δι᾽ ἀρετήν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Menexenus” 237a; τινά τι 5th c.BC: Agatho Tragicus 12; πλεῖστα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.4.6.}}
**II** _passive_, **make merry, enjoy oneself**, εὐφραίνεσθαι ἕκηλον {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.311, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.9, NT.Luke.16.19, etc.}}; τινι **at** or **in** a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 9.16, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 561, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 796b; ἐπί τινι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 5, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 7.5, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.191; ἔν τινι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 1.16; διά τινος [prev. work] 8; ἀπό τινος [prev. work] 4.6: with}}_participle_, εὐφράνθη ἰδών **was rejoiced at** seeing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.62, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 68; εἰ πεπαυμένος μηδέν τι μᾶλλον ἢ νοσῶν εὐφραίνεται 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 280, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 36}}; τὰ ἐμὰ εὐ. **enjoy a pleasure** in my stead, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMar.” 13.2.}}

2167:
** εὐφροσύνη**,
_Epic dialect_ ἐϋφρ-, ἡ, (εὔφρων) **mirth, merriment**, γέλω τε καὶ εὐφροσύνην παρέχουσαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.8, compare 10.465, etc.}}; especially of a banquet, **good cheer, festivily**, οὐ.. τί φημι χαριέστερον εἶναι ἢ ὅτ᾽ ἐϋφροσύνη μὲν ἔχῃ κατὰ δῆμον ἅπαντα κτλ{{Refs 6th c.BC+|. 9.6, compare “hymnus ad Mercurium” 449, 482, etc.; κρατὴρ μεστὸς ἐϋφροσύνης 6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 1.4: _plural_, σφισι θυμὸς αἰὲν ἐϋφροσυνῃσιν ἰαίνεται}} is cheered **with glad thoughts**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.156}}; **festivities**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 539, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 377 (both Lyric poetry), etc.: chiefly poetry, used by 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.1.32, “Ages.” 9.4 (_plural_): in _sing._, [prev. author] “Cyr.” 3.3.7, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 80b}}; ἡ χαρὰ καὶ ἡ εὐ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 2}}: also in later Prose, {{LXX+NT+4th c.BC+|LXX.Gen.31.27, +others, NT.Act.2.28, 4th-5th c.AD: Diogenianus Paroemiographus 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus 4.50, “PLips.” 119 ii 1 (3rd c.AD), etc.}}; εὐ. ψυχῆς οἶνος πινόμενος {{LXX|LXX.Sir.34.28 (31.36).}}
**II** _proper name_, {{Refs|“Euphrosyne”}}, one of the Graces, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 909, etc.}}

2168:
** εὐχᾰριστ-έω**,
**bestow a favour on, oblige**, τῷ δήμῳ τῷ Δηλίων {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 11 (4).665 (Delos, 3rd c.BC)}}; τινι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 2p.4 (3rd c.BC), “PHib.” 1.66.5 (3rd c.BC).}}
**2** **to be thankful, return thanks**, Decrees cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.92, “IPE” 12.352.14 (Chersonesus, 2nd c.BC)}}; τοῖς Αθηναίοις {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC: Posidonius Historicus 36 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, compare 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.92 W., +others}}; ἐπί τινι or περί τινος **for** a thing, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.72.7, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 16.11, etc.}}; especially to the gods, ἐπὶ τῷ ἐρρῶσθαί σε τοῖς θεοῖς εὐ. {{NT+2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 59.10 (2nd c.BC),cf. LXX.Jdt.8.25, NT.1Cor.1.4, etc.}}:—_passive_, **to be thanked**, ηὐχαρίστηται κεραυνοῖς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιστολαί” 17}}; **to be received with thanks**, {{NT|NT.2Cor.1.11.}}
**3** **pray**, τῷ θεῷ περί τινος {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“PLond.” 2.413, 418 (4th c.AD).}}

2169:
** εὐχᾰριστ-ία**, ἡ,
**thankfulness, gratitude**, Decrees cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.91, “Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 3.67, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.93 W.; τοῦ δήμου “OGI” 227.6 (Didyma, 3rd c.BC)}}; πρός τινα {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.59, “PLond.” 3.1178.25 (2nd c.AD)}}; πρὸς τὸν θεόν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.36.1; ἀπόντι μᾶλλον εὐ. ποίει 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 693.}}
**2** **giving of thanks**, εἰς εὐ. θεοῦ {{NT+1st c.AD+|“SIG” 798.5 (Cyzicus, 1st c.AD),compare 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.60, LXX.Wis.16.28, “Corpus Hermeticum” 1.29, etc.: _plural_, ποιεῖσθαι -ίας NT.1Tim.2.1.}}

2170:
** εὐχᾰριστ-ος**, ον,
**agreeable**, τινι τέχνη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 5.10 (_comparative_)}}; λόγοι {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 2.2.1 (_superlative_)}}; - ότατα καὶ πιθανώτατα εἴρηκε {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 12.28.11}}; εὐχάριστα **acceptable gifts**, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|“AJA” 30.249 (5th c.AD: Cypria)}}. _adverb_ -τως, τελευτᾶν τὸν βίον to die **happily**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32.}}
**II** **grateful, thankful**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.3.49 (_superlative_), “Inscription Prien.” 103.8 (2nd-1st c.BC), NT.Col.3.15, etc.}} _adverb_ -τως, διακεῖσθαι πρός τινα {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.90; ἀποδιδόναι 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.520; τῶν γεγονότων μνημονεύειν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.477f.}}
**III** **beneficent**, θεοί {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 41.13 (2nd c.BC)}}; title of Ptolemy V, {{Refs|“OGI” 90.5 (from Rosetta)}}; τὸ τῆς ψυχῆς εὐ. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 18.28}}; βεβαιωτὴς (-ότης codices) εὐχάριστος, of God, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.128 codices (ἰσχυρότατος conjecture Cohn).}}

2171:
** εὐχή**, ἡ,
(εὔχομαι) **prayer** or **vow**, once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (compare εὖχος, εὐχωλή), ἐπὴν εὐχῇσι λίσῃ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.526, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 419, 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 341, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.31, etc.; θεὸς εὔφρων εἴη.. εὐχαῖς 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 4.15; εὐχὰς ἀνασχεῖν τινι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 636}}; εὐχὴν ἐπιτελέσαι, Latin _vota persolvere_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.86; εὐχὰς ἀποδιδόναι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.2.10; εὐχὴν ἀνέστησεν “SIG” 1142 (Phrygia, 1st-2nd c.AD)}}; εὐχῇ χρῆσθαι, Latin _votis potiri_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 688b}}; κατὰ χιλίων.. εὐχὴν ποιήσασθαι χιμάρων to make **a vow** of a thousand goats, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 661; ἐν θεῶν εὐχαῖσι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 239, etc.; εὐχαὶ πρὸς θεούς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 700b; εὐχὰς εὔχεσθαι τοῖς θεοῖς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.130; εὐχὰς εὔξεται ὑπὲρ τῆς πόλεως “Inscription Prien.” 174.18 (2nd c.BC)}}; εὐχὰς ποιεῖσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.32, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 400a17; εὐχὴν ἀποθύειν 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 43.10}}; κατ᾽ εὐχήν, ἐξ εὐχῆς, Latin _ex voto_, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 48, “Anthologia Graeca” 6.357 (3rd c.BC: Theaetetus Epigrammaticus)}}; ἔχειν εὐχήν to be under **a vow,** {{NT|NT.Acts.18.18.}}
**2** **wish** or **aspiration**, opposed to reality, εὐχαῖς ὅμοια λέγειν to build '**castles in the air**', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 499c, compare 540d; μὴ εὐ. δοκῇ εἶναι ὁ λόγος[prev. work] 450d}}; κατὰ τὴν τῶν παίδων εὐ. like a boy's **wish**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Sph.” 249d}}; εὐχῆς ἄξια things **to be wished**, but not expected, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.182}}; πολιτεία ἡ κατ᾽ εὐχὴν γινομένη the **ideal** state, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1295a29, compare 1288b23; ζῆν κατ᾽ εὐχήν [prev. work] 1260b29.}}
**3** **prayer for evil**, i.e. **curse, imprecation**, πατρὸς κατ᾽ εὐχάς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 820, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 70.}}

2172:
** εὔχομαι**,
_imperfect_ εὐχόμην (_Attic dialect_ ηὐ-) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.275, etc.}}: _future_ εὔξομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 622 (_anapaest meter_), etc.:}}_aorist 1_ εὐξάμην (_Attic dialect_ ηὐ-) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.254, etc.}}: _2nd pers._ _singular_ _subjunctive_ εὔξεαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.45: (augment ηὐ}}- only _Attic dialectaccusative_ to Hdn.{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Gr. 2.789, 2nd c.AD: Moeris Grammaticus 175)}}: —**pray**, θεοῖς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.296, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.64, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.58, etc.; ἀγάλμασι 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 5; ἀνέμοισι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.178; Ἀργείοισι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 980: c._accusative_ _cognate_, εὐχὰς εὔ. τοῖς θεοῖς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.130}}; εὐχὰς ὑπέρ τινος πρὸς τοὺς θεοὺς εὔ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.18}}; εὔ. ἔπος **to utter** it **in prayer**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 37.19, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.2, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 1059 (Lyric poetry)}}; μεγάλα, μέγα εὔ., **pray** aloud, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.275, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.239; πολλὰ Ποσειδάωνι 3.54: later, c._accusative_, Ἄρτεμιν εὔ. “Anthologia Graeca” 9.268 (Antip. Thess.): _absolutely_, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.298, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 465 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 39 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator (Lyric poetry), etc.}}
**2** with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, **pray that**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.353, 21.211, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.31}}; of an unrealizable wish {{Refs 4th c.BC+|compare (εὐχή 2), 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1118a32, compare 3rd c.BC: Macho Comicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 8.341d}}: with _infinitive_ alone, εὔ. θάνατον φυγεῖν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.401}}; τί δοκέεις εὔχεσθαι ἄλλο ἢ.. λαβεῖ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.27; οἶκον ἰδεῖν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.293, etc.}}; τοῖς θεοῖς with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 117c}}; also εὔ. τοὺς θεοὺς δοῦναί μοι **pray** that the gods may give, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 351, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.1.26; πρὸς τοὺς θεοὺς διδόναι [prev. author] “Mem.” 1.3.2; ταῖς Μούσαις εἰπεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 545d, etc.}}; later εὔ. ἵνα{{Refs 1st c.BC+|Aristeas 45, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 9.53, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 2.6.12; ὅπως “Wien.Stud.” 44.159.}}
**3** with _accusative_ _object_, **pray for, long** or **wish for**, χρυσόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 8.37, etc.; εὐχόμενος ἄν τις ταῦτα εὔξαιτο 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.1}}; εὔ. τινί τι **pray for** something **for** a person, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1019; κακόν τινι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 21.21}}; also, **pray for** a thing **from**.., τοῖς θεοῖς πολλὰ ἀγαθὰ ὑπέρ τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.2.10}}; τοῖς θεοῖς πολυκαρπίαν{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work] 3.14.3; δεινὸν κατά τινος 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Abd.” 32.}}
**II** **vow** or **promise to** do.., with _future_ _infinitive_, εὔχομαι ἐξελάαν κύνας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.526; θεοῖσι.. ἑκατόμβας ῥέξειν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.50, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.101, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 58b, “IG” 12.108.55, 22.112.6 (4th c.BC): with}}_aorist_ _infinitive_, εὔχετο πάντ᾽ ἀποδοῦναι **claimed** (the right) to pay in full, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.499 (unless in signification 111.3): with}}_present_ _infinitive_, ηὔξω θεοῖς.. ἂν ὧδ᾽ ἔρδειν τάδ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 933, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1032 codices}}
**II.2** with _accusative_ _of things_, **vow** a thing, πολλῶν πατησμὸν εἱμάτων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 963; ἱερεῖον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1619; [λύχνον] περὶ παιδός 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 56.3.}}
**II.3** εὔ. κατά τινος of the thing vowed (as though on the altar), εὔ. τοῖς θεοῖς κατὰ ἑκατόμβης {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Marius” 26, compare 2.294b; κατὰ νικητηρίων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Epistulae” 1.16.}}
**III** **profess loudly, boast, vaunt**, οὕτω φησὶ καὶ εὔχεται, οὕνεκ᾽ Ἀχιλλεὺς νηυσὶν ἔπι γλαφυρῇσι μένει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.366; εὑρεῖν 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 2.6}}: mostly, not of empty boasting, but of something of which one has a right to be proud, ταύτης τοι γενεῆς τε καὶ αἵματος εὔχομαι εἶναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.211, compare 8.190; πατρὸς δ᾽ ἐξ ἀγαθοῦ καὶ ἐγὼ γένος εὔχομαι εἶναι 14.113, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 449a: rarely without}}_infinitive_, ἐκ Κρητάων γένος εὔχομαι (i.e. εἶναι) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.199; τὸ πατρόθεν ἐκ Διὸς εὔχονται 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.23, compare “P.” 4.97}}; πόρτις εὔχεται βοός (i.e. εἶναι) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 314; ἔνθεν εὔχομαι γένος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 696}}; but also,
**III.2** **boast vainlv, brag**, εὔχεαι αὔτως {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.388}}: with _infinitive_, εὔ. δῃώσειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1318.}}
**III.3** simply, **profess** or **declare**, ἱκέτης δέ τοι εὔ. εἶναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.450; οὔτ᾽ ὦν ἀκοῦσαι οὔτ᾽ ἰδεῖν εὔχοντο 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.53}}; τίς χθὼν εὔχεται ἥδε [εἶναι]; {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.1251; compare above 11.1.}}
**IV** _passive_, ἐμοὶ μετρίως ηὖκται I **have prayed** sufficiently, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 279c}}: _perfect_ _infinitive_, ταῦτα μὲν ηὖχθαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 22.112.12 (4th c.BC)}}; ἡ πανήγυρις ἡ.. εὐχθεῖσα **vowed**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 48.32: but}}_pluperfect_ (or non-thematic preterite) ηὔγμην in _active_ sense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 610; so εὖκτο Thebaïs “fragment” 3}}. (Cf. Sanskrit _óhate_ 'to (be able to) boast that one is', 'to brag', Avest. **aog**- 'declare solemnly'.)

2173:
** εὔχρηστ-ος**, ον,
also -η, ον {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Fragmenta” 272}}: (χράομαι):— **useful, serviceable**, ἔν τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 16 (_superlative_)}}; πρός τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 777b, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.8.5, etc.; εἴς τι 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 5.40 (_superlative_)}}; of persons, with _dative_, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 3p.153 (3rd c.BC)}}; τῷ δήμῳ {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Inscription Prien.” 102.5 (2nd-1st c.BC)}}; σκεῦος εὔ. τῷ δεσπότῃ {{NT|NT.2Tim.2.21}}; εὔχρησται ἡμέραι, in astrology, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Fragmenta” 272}}. _adverb_ -τως {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.334; εὐ. ἔχειν πρός τι 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.73.5, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Tactica - Griechische Kriegsschriftsteller” 3.2.}}
**II** **easy to execute**, διίππευσις {{Refs|[prev. work] 18.4.}}

2174:
** εὐψῡχ-έω**,
**to be of good courage,** {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Phil.2.19, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 11.6.9, “BGU” 1097.15 (1st c.AD), 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 3.135.}}
**II** εὐψύχει **farewell**! a common _inscription_ on tombs, {{Refs|“IG” 12(2).393 (from Mytilene), etc.}}
**II.2** εὐψυχεῖν, ={χαίρειν}, in a letter of condolence, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 115.1 (2nd c.AD).}}

2176:
** εὐώνυμ-ος**, ον,
(ὄνομα)
**A** **of good name, honoured**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 409, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.7, etc.}}; εὐ. χάρις the honour **of a good name**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “P.” 11.58}}; δίκη.. μὴ εὐ. not **creditable**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 754e.}}
**A.2** **expressed in well-chosen terms**, λόγος {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Lex.” 1.}}
**A.II** **having an auspicious name** or **sound**, ἀριστοκρατία {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 302d; πρόσρημα 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 52.4.}}
**A.II.2** **prosperous, fortunate**, δίκα, πόδες, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.48, 8.47, compare 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 895.37.}}
**A.II.3** epithet of Artemis, Ἀρχ. Ἐφ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1914.20 (Gonni, 4th-3rd c.BC).}}
**A.III** euphemistic (like ἀριστερός) for **left, on the left hand** (because **bad** omens **came from the left**), ὠλένη εὐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 926; ἐξ εὐωνύμου χειρός 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.109}}; ἐξ εὐωνύμου (i.e. χειρός) {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.72}}; κατὰ τὰ εὐ. {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 11.10; εἰς τὰ εὐ. παρεκκλίνειν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 666b7; ἐπὶ τὰ εὐ. ἀνακλίνεσθαι [prev. author] “HA” 498a11; ἐξ -ωνύμων NT.Matt.20.21}}; as military term, τὸ εὐ. κέρας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.111, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.67, etc.}}; τὸ εὐ. (without κέρας) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.96.}}
**A.III.2** euphemistic of **bad** omens, opposed to οἱ δεξιοὶ φύσιν, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 490, compare “SIG” 1167.3 (Ephesus, 6th-5th c.BC).}}
**A.III.3** Astron., **southerly**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Cleomedes Astronomus 1.1.}}

2178:
** ἐφάπαξ** [ᾰπ],
_adverb_ **once for all**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 175, NT.Rom.6.10, NT.Heb.7.27, etc.}}
**II** **at once, **{{NT|NT.1Cor.15.6.}}

2183:
** ἐφημερ-ία**, ἡ,
**division** of the priests **for the daily service of the temple**, {{LXX+NT|LXX.1Ch.23.6, LXX.Neh.23.30, NT.Luke.1.5.}}
**2** the **service** itself, {{LXX|LXX.1Esd.1.16.}}

2184:
** ἐφήμερ-ος**, ον,
_Doric dialect_ ἐπάμ- {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 8.95, “fragment” 182}} (**ἐφαμ-** {{Refs|“I.” 7(6).40, “fragment” 157)}}: (ἡμέρα):—more common form of ἐφημέριος, especially in Prose, **living but a day**: hence, **short-lived**, τερπνόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “I.” [same place]; τύχαι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 866, 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 45; ὄλβος οὐ βέβαιος, ἀλλ᾽ ἐ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” [558]; ἐ. σώματα καὶ χρήματα ἡγεῖσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.53; χρῆσις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1252b16; ἐ. καὶ προπετῆ βίον 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 382; τὸ ἐ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1096b5; ἐ. πᾶν τὸ τῶν πολλῶν ἀγαθόν 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 489.}}
**2** of men, ἐφήμεροι **creatures of a day**, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 8.95, 7th-6th c.BC(?): Semonides Iambographus 1.3, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 83; ὦ τάλας ἐφάμερε 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 157; ὦφήμερε 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 223; ὦ φίλοι καὶ ἀτεχνῶς ἐ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 923a.}}
**II** **for the day, daily**, πυρετός {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 4.55; τροφή 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 8.41, NT.Jam.2.15, 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 62.17. compare 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 3.32 (_plural_); γυμνασία 1st c.BC: Asclepiodotus Tacticus “Tact.” 1.4; πράξεις 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Pseudol.” 17; δαπάνη 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pericles” 16, etc.}}: _neuter_ _plural_ as _adverb_, **once a day**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus “Eup.” 1.9.}}
**III** φάρμακον ἐ. **killing on the same day**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Themistocles” 31.}}

2185:
** ἐφικνέομαι**,
_Ionic dialect_ ἐπ-, _future_ ἐφίξυμαι {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 6.3:}}_aorist 2_ ἐφῑκόμην, _Epic dialect_ -ῐκόμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.613}}: _perfect_ ἐφῖγμαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.101:}}
**I** **reach at, aim at**, with _genitive_, of two combatants, ἅμα δ᾽ ἀλλήλων ἐφίκοντο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.613}}; simply, **reach** or **hit** with a stick, εὐ μάλα μου ἐφικέσθαι πειράσεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 292a; ὅσων ἂν ἐφικέσθαι δυνηθῶσιν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.227; ἐφῖκται πάντων ἡ τούτου κακοπραγμοσύνη 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.101, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.267c, etc.; σφενδόνῃ οὐκ ἂν ἐφικοίμην αὐτόσε 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 55.20; τὰ βέλη ἐ. ἄχρι πρὸς τὸν σκοπόν 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nigr.” 36.}}
**I.2** **reach, extend**, ὅσον ὁ ἥλιος ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 1. 7.1, etc.; ἐφ᾽ ὅσον ἀνθρώπων μνήμη ἐ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.5.8}}; ἐ. ἐπὶ τοσαύτην γῆν τῷ ἀφ᾽ ἑαυτοῦ φόβῳ **to reach** by the terror of his name over.., {{Refs|[prev. work] 1.1.5}}; ἐ. ἐς τὸ λεπτότατον **to reach** to the smallest matter, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “JConf.” 19; ὅπου μὴ ἐ. ἡ λεοντῆ, προσραπτέον.. τὴν ἀλωπεκῆν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.190e}}; with _participle_, ἐ. φθεγγόμενον {{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. author] “TG” 18; ἐ. βλέποντα μέχρι τινός 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 62.1.}}
**I.3** _metaphorically_, **hit, touch the right points**, ἐ. ἐξαριθμούμενος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.57.3; τὰ ἄλλα λέγων ἐπίκεο ἀληθέστατα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.9.}}
**I.4** **reach, attain to**, τῆς ἀρετῆς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.5; ἀνδραγαθίας 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.189; τοῦ τριηραρχεῖν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.28, compare 122; τῷ λόγῳ τῶν ἐκεῖ κακῶν [prev. author] 19.65: with}}_infinitive_, ἐ. τῷ λόγῳ διελθεῖν **to be able to**.., {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.338c, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.4.11, “Inscription Prien.” 105.47 (1st c.BC)}}: _absolutely_, **succeed in one's projects**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Μιθριδάτειος” 102}}; of a poison, **reach** a vital part, **take effect**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 111.}}
**II** with _accusative_, **to come upon**, like{ἐφικάνω}, εἴ σε μοῖρ᾽ ἐφίκοιτο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 5(4).15: with double _accusative_, ἐπικέσθαι μάστιγι πληγὰς τὸν Ἑλλήσποντον}} **to visit** it **with** blows, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.35.}}
**III** with _accusative_, **befit, be suitable to** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare (ἱκνέομαι 111), 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 17.}}

2186:
** ἐφίστημι**,
_Ionic dialect_ **ἐπ-**:
**A** causal in _present_, _imperfect_, _future_, and _aorist 1_ (also in the later _perfect_ and _pluperfect_ ἐφέστᾰκα, ἐφεστάκειν [ᾰ], see below 11.1, VI. 2):
**A.I** **set, place upon**, τεῖχος τείχει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.75; τι ἐπί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 116a; τι ἐπί τινι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.1.7; ὅρους ἐπὶ οἰκίαν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 41.6: _metaphorically_, ἐ. τὴν ἐκεῖ μοῖραν βίῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 498c; ἀνάγκην τινί 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.16.}}
**A.II** **set over**, μ᾽ Ἀπόλλων τῷδ᾽ ἐπέστησεν τέλει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1202; φύλακ᾽ ἐπέστησεν βοτ [prev. author] “Supp.” 303; ἐ. τινὰ ὕπαρχόν τισι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.27; τινὰ παιδαγωγόν τινι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.122b, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 2.1; τινὰ πεντηκοντόρῳ [prev. author] “An.” 5.1.15; τινὰ τοῖς πράγμασι 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.27; τὸν νόμον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1292b28; ἐπὶ [συμμάχων] τινά 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.65.9; ἐφεστάκει τινὰς πρὸς χρείαν [prev. author] 10.20.5; κύνα ἐπὶ ποίμνην 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 26.22; τινὰ ἐπὶ τὰς εὐθύνας [prev. author] 18.112}}: with _infinitive_, βουλὴν ἐπιμελεῖσθαι τῆς εὐκοσμίας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 7.37}}:—_passive_, **to be appointed, instituted,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|PTeb. 61 (b).358 (2nd c.BC), etc.}}
**A.II.2** **bring in**, ἡ τύχη ἐπιστήσασα Ῥωμαίους {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 15.20.6}}; Φίλιππον ἐ. τοῖς πράγμασι **to let** him **have a hand in** the business, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.34.}}
**A.II.3** **bring in, cause, occasion**, κατάπληξίν τισι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 14.62}}; κίνδυνον, ἀγῶνά τινι, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Ἀννιβαϊκή” 55, “Syr.” 10; ἡ τύχη λοιμικὴν διάθεσιν ἐπέστησε Γαλάταις 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.20.7.}}
**A.III** **set up, establish**, ἀγῶνα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.167, 6.38}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, **ordain, prescribe**, ὁ νόμος ἐφίστησι τὰ λοιπὰ κρίνειν τοὺς ἄρχοντας {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1287a26}}; ἐπιστήσατε quid facere debeamus, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Plinius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor “Ep.” 6.31.12.}}
**A.IV** **set by** or **near to**, ἐπιστήσαντες κύκλῳ τὸ σῆμα ἱππέας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.72}}; especially **place in rear**, of troops, τὴν φάλαγγα τούτοις κατόπιν ἐ. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.33.6, compare 1.26. 14.}}
**A.V** **stop, cause to halt**, ἐπιστῆσαι τὸ στράτευμα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.2.18}}; τὴν ὁδόν, τὴν πορείαν, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.112, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cimon” 1; τοὺς ἱππέας τοῦ πρόσω 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 5.16.1}}; ἐ. τὴν ὁρμήν **check** it, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 16.34.2}}; τὴν διήγησιν **interrupt** it, {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.11.1}}; **check**, ἔμμηνα {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.125, compare “POxy.” 1088.20 (1st c.AD): _absolutely_, ἐπιστήσας (i.e. ἑαυτόν)}} **having halted**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.8.15}}:—_passive_, **to be checked, stopped,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|PPetr. 2p.62 (3rd c.BC)}}; ἐὰν ἐφίστηται ἡ κοιλία {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.122.}}
**A.VI** ἐφίστημι τὴν διάνοιαν κατά τι, περί τινος, **fix one's** mind upon it, **attend** to it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.69, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 987b3, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Char.Prooem.”, etc.; τὴν σκέψιν περί τινος 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1090a2; τὸν λόγον [prev. author] “Juv.” 470b5; τὸν νοῦν τινι 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 12.1; αὑτὸν ἐπιστήσας ἐπί τι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 135a26: ἐπιστῆσαι}} _absolutely_, **give attention**, τούτοις ἐπιστήσαντες {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. author] “Mu.” 391a26; περί τινος [prev. author] “GC” 315b18; περί τι [prev. author] “HA” 487a13; ἐπί τι 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.65.5, etc.}}; ἐπιστήσασι μᾶλλον λεκτέον one must speak **with** more **care and accuracy**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1335b3, compare “EN” 1144a22; πότερον.. ἤ 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator}}**ad Them.**265b; ὅτι.. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.97 (hence ἐπίσταμαι, ἐπιστήμη, which see).}}
**A.VI.2** with _accusative_ _person_, **arrest the attention of**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Tiberius Gracchus” 17, compare 2.17e, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(2).105}}; ἐπιστῆσαί τινα ἐπί τι **call** his **attention** to, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.61.11, compare 4.34.9}}; τοῦ καιροῦ τοῦ κατὰ τὴν διήγησιν ἐφεστακότος ἡμᾶς ἐπί τι **having led** us to.., {{Refs|[prev. author] 10.21.2, compare 31.23.1}}: hence, **object**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 1.4.5.}}
**B** _intransitive_ in _middle_ and _passive_, ἐφίσταμαι, _aorist 1_ ἐπεστάθην [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus] “fragment” [1127.5], 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 819, “IT” 1375, etc.}}, with _perfect_, _pluperfect_ (_Aeolic dialect_ _pluperfect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἐπήστᾱκε {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Schwyzer” 646.16 (Cyme, 2nd c.BC): _Doric dialect_}}_pluperfect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἐφεστάκεον [ᾱ] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 241.146 (Delph., 4th c.BC))}}, and _aorist 2_ _active_: (the causal tenses are not found in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, the _middle_ or _passive_ only in _imperfect_ ἐφίστατο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.644}}; elsewhere always _aorist 2_ or _perfect_ _active_ with _Epic dialect_ _infinitive_ ἐφεστάμεναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.380)}}:—**stand upon**, τεῖχος.. ῥύατ᾽ ἐφεσταότες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.515; πύργῳ ἐφεστήκει 6.373; δίφρῳ ἐφεσταότος 17.609, etc.; ἐπέστη βηλῷ ἔπι λιθέῳ 23.201; ἡ.. ἐπισταθεῖσα ὀρθή 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1051a28; ἐπὶ τὰς.. σχεδίας 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.46.8.}}
**B.2** **to be imposed upon**, μόχθων τῶν ἐφεστώτων ἐμοί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1170.}}
**B.3** **stand on the top** or **surface**, τὸ ἐπιστάμενον [τοῦ γάλακτος], i. e. **cream**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.2; λιπαρότητες ἄνω ἐφιστάμεναι 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προγνωστικόν” 12}}; ἐ. καθάπερ ὀρρὸς [γάλακτι] {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.72}}; of vapour, **form**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Juventute” 469b31.}}
**B.II** **to be set over**, ἐφίσταται πύλαις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 538; οἷός τε πολλοῖς προβατίοις ἐφεστάναι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 955; οἷοι νῷν ἐφεστᾶσι σκοποί 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 945; ἄρχοντες ἐφ᾽ ἑκάστῳ μέρει ἐ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 9.5; ἐπί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 460b; ἐπὶ τῆς πολιτείας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.298: rarely with _genitive_, τὸν ἐπεστεῶτα τῆς διώρυχος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.117; ὅσοι θεοῦ χρημάτων ἐφέστασαν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 1098: _absolutely_ in}}_participle_, ὁ ἐφεστηκώς the **person in authority**, the **officer in command**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 21.9}}; οἱ ἐφεστῶτες, _Ionic dialect_ οἱ ἐπεστεῶτες, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.148, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1072, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.5.19.}}
**B.III** **stand by** or **near**, ὣς πυκνοὶ ἐφέστασαν ἀλλήλοισιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.133; ἐπ᾽ ἄκρῳ χείλει ἐφεσταότες, ἐ. παρὰ τάφρῳ, 12.52, 199; θύρῃσιν ἐφίστατο 11.644}}; ἐπὶ τὰς πύλας, ἐπὶ τὰς θύρας, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.77, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 212d; ἐπὶ τοῖς προθύροις [prev. author] “Phlb.” 64c}}; especially of dreams or visions, **appear to**, εὕδοντι ἐπέστη ὄνειρος {{NT+8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.34, compare 7.14; ὄναρ κεφαλῆφιν ἐπέστη 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.496; ἐπιστᾶσα τῆς νυκτός 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 10.65; ἄγγελος ἐπέστη αὐτοῖς NT.Luke.2.9}}: _absolutely_, **stand by**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.78; πολλῶν ἐφεστώτων 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Συριακή” 10; ἤμην ἐφεστώς NT.Act.22.20; οἱ λέβητες ἐπεστεῶτες 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.59; ὁ ἀντίδικος ἐφέστηκε 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 172e, compare 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.79}}; without hostile sense, ἐπέστης {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 558, cf. NT.Luke.2.38, etc.}}; of troops, **to be posted after** or **behind**, κατόπιν ἐ. τοῖς θηρίοις {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 16.18.7.}}
**B.III.2** in hostile sense, **stand against**, τὰ φρονέοντες ἐφέστασαν ἀλλήλοισιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.703, compare 5.624; ἔνθα μένος πνείοντες ἐφέστασαν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.203, compare 24.380}}; **appear before**, of an army, ἐπὶ τῇ πόλι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.203; ἐπὶ τὸ βασίλειον 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.58}}; **come upon suddenly** or **by surprise**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.69; ἐξαίφνης ἐπιστὰς τοῖς γιγνομένοις 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.41, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 6.5, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DDeor.” 17.1; εἰς τοὺς ὄχλους 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 18.9}}; so of events, etc., αἰφνίδιος αὐτοῖς ἐ. ὄλεθρος {{NT+2nd c.BC+|NT.1Thes.5.3, cf. NT.Luke.21.34; διὰ τὸν ἐφεστῶτα ζόφον 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.20.7; διὰ τὸν ὑετὸν τὸν ἐφεστῶτα NT.Act.28.2.}}
**B.III.3** _metaphorically_, of events, **spring upon** one, **occur**, πρίν μοι τύχη τοιάδ᾽ ἐπέστη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 777, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.82}}; in _perfect_, **impend, be at hand**, τὸν ἐφεστηκότα κίνδυνον τῇ πόλει {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.176; ὁ καιρός.. ἐφέστηκε NT.2Tim.4.6; περὶ τοῦ βασιλέως.. ὁ λόγος ἐφέστηκε νῦν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1287a2, compare “Metaphorical” 999a25}}; of a more remote future, **to be in store, lie in wait for**, κῆρες ἐφεστᾶσιν θανάτοιο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.326.}}
**B.IV** **halt, stop**, as in a march, ἄλλοτε καὶ ἄλλοτε ἐφιστάμενος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.4.26 (compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus V); ἐπιστὰς περιέμεινα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 172a: with _genitive_, ἐ. τοῦ πλοῦ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.91.}}
**B.V** **fix one's mind on, give one's attention to**, σφαγῇ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 547; τῇ τρύγῃ “PFlor.” 236.4 (3rd c.AD)}}; ἐπί τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 10.29, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.60; τοῖς πράγμασιν.. ἐπιστάντες [prev. author] 4.12}}; ἐπιστάς _absolutely_ (i.e. τοῖς πράγμασι), {{Refs|[prev. author] 18.233; διὰ ταῦτ᾽ ἐγρήγορεν, ἐφέστηκεν [prev. author] 6.19.}}
**C** _aorist 1_ _middle_ in causal sense, **set up**, τὰς θύρας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 8.7}}; **set, post**, φρουροὺς ἐπεστησάμην {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 8.2.19}}; τέλος ἐπιστήσασθαι, Latin _finem imponere_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 802a:}}_present_ is once so used, τοῦ με τήνδ᾽ ἐφίστασαι βάσι; why dost thou **cause** me **to halt**? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 339.}}
**C.2** ἐπιστησάμενος, _intransitive_, **having been** ἐπιστάτης, {{Refs|“IGRom.” 4.1265 (from Thyatira).}}

2190:
** ἐχθρός**, ά, όν,
(ἔχθος) **hated, hateful**, of persons and things, frequently from {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus downwards (8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus has it only in this _passive_ sense)}}; ἐ. γάρ μοι κεῖνος ὁμῶς Ἀΐδαο πύλῃσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.156, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.312}}; ἐχθρὸν δέ μοί ἐστιν c._infinitive_, 'tis **hateful** to me to.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.452; θεοῖσιν ἐ. 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 766, 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 601, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 34; ὁ θεοῖσιν ἐ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 74, etc.}}; compare θεοισεχθρός.
**II** _active_, **hating, hostile**, first in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus and 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus (see. below 111), τινι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 10.11, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 6.1, etc.: with _genitive_, ὕβριος ἐχθρὰν ὁδόν}} **averse from** insolence, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.90: _absolutely_, ἐ. γλῶσσα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 309 (_anapaest meter_); ὀργαί [prev. author] “Eu.” 937 (_anapaest meter_), etc.; ἀστέρες 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 143.5.}}
**III** as _substantive_, ἐχθρός, ὁ, **enemy**, where the _active_ and _passive_ senses frequently coincide, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 342, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.84, etc.; ἀνὴρ ἐ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.92; ὁ Διὸς ἐ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 120 (_anapaest meter_); ἐχθροῖς ἐχθρὰ πορσύνων [prev. author] “Ag.” 1374; εἴ.. τινα ἴδοι ἐχθρὸν ἑαυτοῦ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.47; οἱ ἐμοὶ ἐ. [prev. author] 6.89, etc.—Acc. to 1st-2nd c.AD: Ammonius Grammaticus “περὶ ὁμοίων καὶ διαφόρων λέξεων” p.63 V., ἐχθρός}} is **one who has been** φίλος, **but is alienated**; πολέμιος **one who is at war**; δυσμενής **one who has long been alienated and refuses to be reconciled.**
**IV** regular _comparative_ ἐχθρότερος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Prooemia” 40.3, “Anthologia Graeca” 5.160 (3rd c.BC: Hedylus Epigrammaticus or 3rd c.BC: Asclepiades Epigrammaticus)}}; _superlative_ -ότατος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 1.65, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1346 (Lyric poetry), 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.300: but more frequently irregular ἐχθίων, ἔχθιστος (which see).}}
**V** _adverb_ ἐχθρῶς, μισοῦντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 697d, etc.}}: _comparative_ -οτέρως {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 5.18}}: _superlative_ -ότατα {{Refs|[prev. author] 23.149.}}

2191:
** ἔχιδν-α**, ἡ,
(ἔχις) **viper**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.108, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 771, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 218a, etc.}}; probably of a **constrictor snake,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|NT.Acts.28.3: _metaphorically_, of a treacherous wife or friend, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 249, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 531}}; ἱματισμένη ἔ., of woman, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Secundus Gnomologus “Sententiae - FPG” 8}}; γεννήματα ἐχιδνῶν brood **of vipers**, term of reproach, in {{NT|NT.Matt.3.7.}}
**II** _proper name_ of a monster, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 297, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1099.}}

2192:
** ἔχω**,
**A**_2nd pers._ _singular_ ἔχεισθα conjecture in {{Refs 7th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1316 (ἔχοισθα codex), ἔχῃσθα conjecture in 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 21 (ἔχεισθα codex)}}; _2nd pers._ _singular_ _subjunctive_ ἔχῃσθα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.180:}}_imperfect_ εἶχον, _Epic dialect_ ἔχον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.22, +others}}, _Ionic dialect_ and _poetry_ ἔχεσκον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.257, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.12, “epigram Gr.” 988.6 (from Balbilla):}}_future_ ἕξω, _Epic dialect_ _infinitive_ ἑξέμεναι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Aetia” 3.1.27 (of duration) or σχήσω}} (of momentary action, especially in sense **check**, see below {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 11.9}}, not found in _Attic dialect_ Inscrr. or {{Refs|“NT”)}}; _2nd pers._ _singular_ σχήσησθα {{Refs|“hymnus ad Cererem” 366 codices:}}_aorist 1_ ἔσχης α _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 17.177, also ἔσχα “IG” 3.1363.6, 14.1728, _3rd.pers._ _plural_ μετ-έσχαν [prev. work]12(7).271.12 (Amorgos, 3rd c.AD):}}_aorist 2_ ἔσχον, _imperative_ σχές {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1013, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1353 (_anapaest meter_) (σχέ}} only in Oracle texts cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 638 (uncertain reading), sometimes in compounds in codices, as πάρασχε 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 842, κάτασχε [prev. author] “HF” 1210)}}; _subjunctive_ σχῶ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.309, etc.; _optative_ σχοίην 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 1.45, in compounds σχοῖμι (as μετάσχοιμι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1484 (Lyric poetry), κατάσχοιμεν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.11)}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ σχοίησαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “pro Euxenippo” 32, σχοῖεν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.33}}; _infinitive_ σχεῖν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.520, etc.}}, _Epic dialect_ σχέμεν {{Refs|8.254}} (in Alexandr. Gr. _3rd.pers._ _plural_ _imperfect_ and _aorist 2_ εἴχοσαν {{NT+3rd c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 5.208 (3rd c.BC: Posidippus Comicus or 3rd c.BC: Asclepiades Epigrammaticus), variant in NT.John.15.22, ἔσχοσαν 2nd c.BC: Scymnus Geographus 695): for the poetry form ἔσχεθον}}, see at {Σχέθω}: _perfect_ ἔσχηκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 765a, εἴσχηκα in Inscrr. of 3rd-1st c.BC, “SIG” 679.54, etc.}}; _Epic dialect_ ὄχωκα is uncertain, see at {συνόχωκα}:—_middle_, _imperfect_ εἰχόμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.244, etc.}}: _future_ ἕξομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.102, etc.}}; σχήσομαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 235, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1335, more frequently in compounds (ἀνα-) 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 252, (παρα-) 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 9.8, etc.:}}_perfect_ _passive_ παρ-έσχημαι in _middle_ sense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.6.11, etc.}}: _aorist 2_ ἐσχόμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.85}}, rare in _Attic dialect_ except in compounds; _imperative_ σχέο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.379, σχέσθε 22.416, later σχοῦ in compounds (ἀνά-) 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}}**lon**947, etc.; _infinitive_ σχέσθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.422, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Fragmenta” 79:}} —_passive_, _future_ _middle_ ἐν-έξομαι in _passive_ sense, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 516, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 51.11, later σχεθήσομαι 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 15.3}}, frequently in compounds (συ-) {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.83 W., (ἐν-) 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.98 of, (ἐπι-) 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 1.186}}: _aorist 1_ ἐσχέθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Anabasis” 5.7.4, 6.11.2, 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων ὀξέων παθῶν” 2.5, (κατ-, συν-) 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Solon” 21, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τῶν ἐντὸς παθῶν” 45 vulgar form:}}_future_ _middle_ σχήσομαι in _passive_ sense, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.235 (uncertain), 655, 13.630}}: _aorist 2_ _middle_ in _passive_ sense, ἐσχόμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.696, +others, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.31 (σχέτο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.248, 21.345)}}, _participle_ σχόμενος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.279, probably in 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 19.11, (κατα-) 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.10, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 244e, 1st c.BC: Parthenius Mythographus 33.2 (assuming variant)}}: _perfect_ ἔσχημαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 4.21.2}}; also in compounds, frequently written -ίσχημαι, -ήσχημαι in codices of late authors. (I.-{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} **seĝh**- (cf. Sanskrit _sáhate_ 'overpower', Gothic _sigis_ 'victory', Gr. ἔχ- _dissimilated_ from ἔχ-), reduced form **sĝh**-(σχ-), whence _reduplicate_ ἴσχω (= **si-sĝh-o**) (which see): compare ἕκ-τωρ, ἕξω, ἕξι; but hέχ- {{Refs|“IG” 12.374.161, +others, is a mere error (ἔχ- [prev. work]12.116.4, 16).)}}
**A** Trans., **have, hold**:
**A.I** **possess**, of property, the most common usage, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.336, 16.386, etc.}}; οἵ τι ἔχοντες the **propertied class**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.22}}; ὁ ἔχων a **wealthy man**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 157 (_anapaest meter_); οἱ ἔχοντες 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 57, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1295, “Pl.” 596}}; οἱ οὐκ ἔχοντες the **poor**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 240; κακὸν τὸ μὴ 'χειν [prev. author] “Ph.” 405}}; ἔχειν χρέα **to have** debts **due to one**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 36.41, compare 37.12}}; **to have received**, θεῶν ἄπο κάλλος ἐ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Venerem” 77; τι ἔκ τινος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1618; παρά τινος [prev. author] “Aj.” 663; πρός τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.6.33, etc.; ὑπὸ.. θεοῖσι “hymnus ad Apollinem” 191}}; πλέον, ἔλασσον ἔ.. (see. _Hymns of _ vv.): in _aorist_, **acquire, get**, ὄνομα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 997}}: also _future_ σχήσω, δύναμιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.6; λέχος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 30, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 9.116}}:—_passive_, **to be possessed**, ἔντεα.. μετὰ Τρώεσσιν ἔχονται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.130, compare 197.}}
**A.I.2** **keep, have charge of**, ἔχον πατρώϊα ἔργα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.22; κῆπον 4.737; Εἰλείθυιαι.. ὠδῖνας ἔχουσαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.271; πύλαι.., ἃς ἔχον Ὧραι 5.749, 8.393; τὰς ἀγέλας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.3.7}}; διαιτητῶν ἐχόντων τὰς δίκας **having control of**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 47.45}}; **to be engaged in**, φυλακὰς ἔχον **kept** watch, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.1, 471; σκοπιὴν ἔχεν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.302; ἀλαοσκοπιὴν εἶχε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.515, 13.10}}; σκοπιὴν ἔ. τινός **for** a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.13; δυσμενῶν θήραν ἔχων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 564, etc.}}; ἐν χερσὶν ἔ. τι (see. χείρ).
**A.I.2.b** _metaphorically_, of a patient, οὐκ ἔχει ἑωυτόν **is** not **himself**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τῶν ἐντὸς παθῶν” 49.}}
**A.I.3** with _accusative_ loci, **inhabit**, οὐρανόν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.267; Ὄλυμπον 5.890}}; **haunt**, [Νύμφαι] ἔχουσ᾽ ὀρέων αἰπεινὰ κάρηνα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.123; Βρόμιος ἔχει τὸν χῶρον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 24}}; especially of tutelary gods and heroes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.74, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.3.24}}; of men, πόλιν καὶ γαῖαν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.177, 195, etc.}}; Θήβας ἔσχον (ἔσχεν codices) **ruled** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 4}}; ἔχεις γὰρ χῶρον **occupiest** it, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 37, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.46}}; in military sense, ἔ. τὸ δεξιόν (with or without κέρας) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.107, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.1.15}}; of beasts, τὰ ὄρη ἔ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyn.” 5.12.}}
**A.I.4** **have to wife** or **as husband** (usually without γυναῖκα, ἄνδρα), οὕνεκ᾽ ἔχεις Ἑλένην καί σφιν γαμβρὸς Διός ἐσσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.569, compare 7.313, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.53, etc.; ἔσχε ἄλλην ἀδελφεήν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.31, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.29; νυμφίον 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Aetia” 3.1.27}}; also of a lover, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.54, “Anthologia Graeca” 5.185 (3rd c.BC: Posidippus Comicus), etc.; ἔχω Λαΐδα, ἀλλ᾽ οὐκ ἔχομαι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristippus Philosophus cited in 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 2.75, compare 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 12.544d}}:—in _passive_, τοῦ περ θυγάτηρ ἔχεθ᾽ Ἕκτορι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.398.}}
**A.I.5** **have in one's house, entertain**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.515, 20.377, “hymnus ad Venerem” 231, 273.}}
**A.I.6** _present_ _participle_ with Verbs, almost, = **with**, ἤϊε ἔχων ταῦτα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.128, compare 2.115; ὃς ἂν ἥκῃ ἔχων στρατόν [prev. author] 7.8.δ᾽,compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.10.}} —Prose use.
**A.I.7** of Place, ἐπ᾽ ἀριστερὰ ἔ. τι **keep** it on one's left, i.e. to keep to the right of it, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.171; ἐπ᾽ ἀριστερὰ χειρὸς ἔ. 5.277}}; ἐν δεξιᾷ, ἐν ἀριστερᾷ ἔ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.106}}; τοὺς οἰκέτας ὑστάτους ἔ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.2.2}}: but in _aorist_, **get**, περιπλώοντες τὴν Λιβύην τὸν ἥλιον ἔσχον ἐς τὰ δεξιά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.42.}}
**A.I.8** of Habits, States, or Conditions, bodily or mental, γῆρας λυγρὸν ἔ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.250; ἀνεκτὸν ἔχει κακόν 20.83; ἕλκος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.517; λύσσαν 9.305; μάχην ἔ. 14.57; ἀρετῆς πέρι δῆριν ἔ. 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.515}}; ὕβριν ἔ. **indulge in**.., {{Refs|1.368}}, etc.; [Ἀφροδίτην[{{Refs 8th c.BC+|] 22.445; [φρένας] ἔ. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.394, etc.; βουλήν 2.344; τλήμονα θυμόν 5.670; τόνδε νόον καὶ θυμὸν ἐνὶ στήθεσσιν ἔχοντες 4.309, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.490 (for later senses of νοῦν ἔχειν, see at {νοῦς}); ἄλγεα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.895, etc.; ἄχεα θυμῷ 3.412; πένθος μετὰ φρεσίν 24.105; πένθος φρεσίν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.219; πόνον.. καὶ ὀϊζύν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.2, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.529; οὐδὲν βίαιον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.15; πρήγματα ἔ. [prev. author] 7.147, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 174b, etc.: in periphrastic phrases, ποθὴν ἔ. τινός, ={ποθεῖν}, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.362; ἐπιδευὲς ἔ. τινός, ={ἐπιδεύεσθαι}, 19.180; ἔ. τέλος, ={τελεῖσθαι}, 18.378}}]; κότον ἔ. τινί, ={κοτεῖσθαι},{{Refs 5th c.BC+|13.517; ἐπιθυμίαν τινός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 1281; φροντίδα τινός [prev. author] “Med.” 1301}}; ἡσυχίην ἔ. **keep** quiet, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.45, etc.}} (_future_ ἡσυχίαν ἕξειν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 47.29, but οὐκ ἔσθ᾽ ὅπως.. ἡ. σχήσει}} **will** not **keep** still **for a moment**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.14)}}; αἰτίαν ἔ. **to be** accused, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.1.8; ὑπό τινος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 99 (but μομφὴν ἔ., ={μέμφεσθαι}, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1069, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 445): in}}]_aorist_, of entering upon a state, ἔσχεν χόλον **conceived** anger, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 5.104}}; ἔχειν τι κατά τινος **have** something against somebody, {{NT|NT.Matt.5.23, NT.Mark.11.25, NT.Rev.2.4; ἔχω τι πρός τινα NT.Act.24.19; ἔχειν πρός τινα NT.2Cor.5.12; ἕξει πρὸς τὸν Θεόν “JRS” 14.85 (from Laodicea)}}: —these phrases are frequently inverted, οὓς ἔχε γῆρας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.515; οὐδὲ Ποσειδάωνα γέλως ἔχε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.344; ἀμηχανίη δ᾽ ἔχε θυμόν 9.295; θάμβος δ᾽ ἔχεν εἰσορόωντας 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.79; σ᾽ αὔτως κλέος ἐσθλὸν ἔχει 17.143; Διὸς αἴσῃ, ἥ μ᾽ ἕξει παρὰ νηυσί 9.609 (unless the antecedent is τιμῆς in 1.608); ὥς σφεας ἡσυχίη τῆς πολιορκίης ἔσχε 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.135; ὄφρα με βίος ἔχῃ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 225 (Lyric poetry): with double _accusative_, φόβος μ᾽ ἔχει φρένας 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 379}}; also of external objects, αἴθρη ἔχει κορυφήν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.76; μιν ἔχεν μένος ἠελίοιο 10.160; σε οἶνος ἔχει φρένας 18.331}}; ἔχῃ βέλος ὀξὺ γυναῖκα, of a woman in travail, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.269}}; λόγος ἔχει τινά with _infinitive_, the story **goes, that**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1573 (Lyric poetry)}}; and so in later Gr., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Demosthenes” 28, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.331, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 3.14, “NA” 5.42, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 13.592e; ὡς ἡ φάτις μιν ἔχει 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.3, compare 5, 26, 9.78 (but also ἔχει φάτιν Διονυσοφάνης θάψαι Μαρδόνιον [prev. author] 9.84; [Κλεισθένης] λόγον ἔχει τὴν Πυθίην ἀναπεῖσαι [prev. author] 5.66)}}; ὡς ἂν λόγος ἔχῃ πρὸς ἀνθρώπους, ὅτι.. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 38}}:—_passive_, ἔχεσθαι κακότητι καὶ ἄλγεσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.182; κωκυτῷ καὶ οἰμωγῇ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.409; ὀργῇ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.141; νούσῳ 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 5.6; ἀγρυπνίῃσι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.129; ὑπὸ πυρετοῦ 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 4.34; ὑπὸ τοῦ ὕδρωπος [prev. author] “Prorrh.” 2.6, ἐν ἀπόρῳ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.25; ἐν συμφοραῖς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 395e.}}
**A.I.9** **possess mentally, understand**, ἵππων δμῆσιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.476; τέχνην 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 770; πάντ᾽ ἔχεις λόγον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 582, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 51; ἔχετε τὸ πρᾶγμα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 789}}; ἔχεις τ; **do you understand**? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 733: _imperative_ ἔχε}} **attend! listen**! {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.109b}}; ἔ. οὖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 129b: with _imperative_, ἔχ᾽, ἀποκάθαιρε 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1193; ἔ. νυν, ἄλειψον [prev. author] “Eq.” 490}}; ἔχεις τοῦτο ἰσχυρῶ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 154a}}; **know of** a thing, μαντικῆς ὁδόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 311}}; τινὰ σωτηρία; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 778 (troch.).}}
**A.I.10** **keep up, maintain**, καναχὴν ἔχε **made** a rattling noise, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.105, 794; βοὴν ἔχον, of flutes and lyres, 18.495.}}
**A.I.11** **involve, admit of**, τά γ᾽ αἰσχρὰ κἀνθάδ᾽ αἰσχύνην ἔχει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 244, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.5; βάσανον 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.31; ταῦτ᾽ ἀπιστίαν, ταῦτ᾽ ὀργὴν ἔχει 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 10.44}}; ἀγανάκτησιν, κατάμεμψιν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.41; τὰ ἀόρατα νοσήματα δυσχερεστέραν ἔχει τὴν θεραπείαν 1st c.AD: Onosander (Onasander) Tacticus 1.15.}}
**A.I.12** of Measure or Value, τὸ Δαμαρέτειον.. εἶχε Ἀττικὰς δραχμὰς δέκα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 11.26; ἔχει τὸ Εὐβοϊκὸν τάλαντον Ἀλεξανδρείους δραχμὰς ἑπτακισχιλίας 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Σικελική” 2.2; χοῖρος ἔχων τὸ ὕψος δύο καὶ ἡμίσους πήχεων 2nd c.BC: Ptolemaeus Euergetes II Historicus 9.}}
**A.I.12.b** Geometry texts, ἡ ἔχουσα τὰ κέντρα the (straight line) **containing** the centres, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ ἰσορροπιῶν” 1.6}}; ὁ κύκλος ἔχων τὸ πολύγωνον the circle **containing (circumscribing**) the polygon, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Sph.Cyl.” 1.23.}}
**A.I.13** with _doubleaccusative_, Ὀρφέα ἄνακτ᾽ ἔχειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 953; Ζῆν᾽ ἔχειν ἐπώμοτον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1188; παιδιὰν ἔ. τὸν ἐκείνου θάνατον Seleucus Grammaticus et Historicus 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 4.155e.}}
**A.II** **hold**:
**A.II.1** **hold**, ἔ. χερσίν, ἐν χερσίν, μετὰ χερσίν, etc., see at {χεί; μετὰ γαμφηλῇσιν ἔ}. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.200; πρόσθεν ἔ. ἀσπίδα [prev. work] 157}}; ὑψοῦ, πασάων ὑπέρ, ὄπιθεν κάρη ἔ.,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|6.509, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.107, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.136}}; ἔ. τινί τι **to hold** it for him, as his helper, {{Refs|9.209, 13.600}}; **uphold**, οὐρανὸν.. κεφαλῇ τε καὶ ἀκαμάτῃσι χέρεσσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 517, 746}}; ἔχει δέ τε κίονας of Atlas, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.53; ἐπ᾽ ὤμων πατέρα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “fragment” 373.}}
**A.II.2** **hold fast**, χειρὸς ἔχων Μενέλαον **holding** him **by** the hand, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.154, compare 16.763, 11.488 (see. infr. C.I)}}; ἔ. τινὰ μέσον **grip** one by the middle, of wrestlers, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1047; ἔχομαι μέσος [prev. author] “Ach.” 571, compare “Eq.” 388, “Ra.” 469: _metaphorically_, ἔ. φρεσί}} **keep** in one's mind, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.33; νῷ ἔ. τινά 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 2b, compare “R.” 490a.}}
**A.II.3** of arms and clothes, **bear, wear**, εἷμα δ᾽ ἔχ᾽ ἀμφ᾽ ὤμοισι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.538, compare 595; παρδαλέην ὤμοισιν ἔ. 3.17; σάκος ὤμῳ 14.376; κυνέην κεφαλῇ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.231; τάδε εἵματ᾽ ἔχω 17.24, compare 573, etc.; στολὴν ἀμφὶ σῶμα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 554, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.26, etc.}}; πολιὰς ἔχω I am grey-haired, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.49}}: _absolutely_, as a category, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Categoriae” 2a3.}}
**A.II.4** of a woman, **to be pregnant**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.41, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 4.21, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1335b18; in full ἐν γαστρὶ ἔ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.32; also πρὸς ἑωυτῇ ἔχειν 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 1.26}}.ιγ.
**A.II.4.b** παῖδα ἔσχεν she **had**, i.e. **bore**, a child, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Nicolaus Damascenus Historicus 11 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}
**A.II.5** **support, sustain**, especially an attack, with _accusative_ _person_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.51, 20.27; cf. B.I. 1, C. 111.}}
**A.II.6** **hold fast, keep close**, ὀχῆες εἶχον [πύλας{{Refs|] 12.456; θύρην ἔχε μοῦνος ἐπιβλής 24.453.}}
**A.II.7** **enclose**, φρένες ἧπαρ ἔχουσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.301; σάρκας τε καὶ ὀστέα ἶνες ἔ. 11.219; τοὺς δ᾽ ἄκραντος ἔχει νύξ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 65 (Lyric poetry)}}; of places, **contain**, θηρῶν οὓς ὅδ᾽ ἔχει χῶρος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1147 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 5.4; [τεῖχος] νῆας ἐντὸς ἔχον 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.8; ὅσσους Κρήτη ἐντὸς ἔχει “hymnus ad Apollinem” 30.}}
**A.II.8** **hold** or **keep in a certain direction**, ὀϊστὸν ἔχε **aimed** it, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.871; more fully χεῖράς τε καὶ ἔγχεα.. ἀντίον ἀλλήλων 5.569}}; of horses or ships, **guide, drive, steer**, πεδίονδ᾽ ἔχον ὠκέας ἵππους {{Refs 8th c.BC+|3.263, compare 11.760; φόβονδε 8.139; τῇ ῥα.. ἔχον ἵππους 5.752, etc.; παρὲξ ἔχε δίφρον 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Scutum Herculis” 352; ὅπῃ ἔσχες.. εὐεργέα νῆα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.279; παρὰ τὴν ἤπειρον ἔ. νέας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.95, etc.: _absolutely_, τῇ ῥ᾽ ἔχε}} that way he **held his course**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.378, compare 23.422}}; Πύλονδ᾽ ἔχον I **held on** to Pylos, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.182, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 720: _metaphorically_, ἐπὶ ῥητορείαν ἔσχε 6th c.AD: Hesychius Milesius Historicus (?)cited in Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 600c}}; also (especially in _future_ σχήσω, _aorist 2_ ἔσχον), **put in, land**, νέες ἔσχον ἐς τὴν Ἀργολίδα χώρην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.92; σχεῖν πρὸς τὴν Σαλαμῖνα [prev. author] 8.40}}; ἐς Φειάν, τῷ Δήλῳ, κατὰ τὸ Ποσειδώνιον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.25, 3.29, 4.129; τάχ᾽ οὖν τις ἄκων ἔσχε 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 305}}; ποῖ σχήσειν δοκεῖ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 188}}; ἔχε.. ἀρὰν ἐπ᾽ ἄλλοις **point** it against others, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1119 (Lyric poetry)}}; ὄμμ᾽ ἔ. **to turn** or **keep** one's eye **fixed**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Aj.” 191 (Lyric poetry); ἐπὶ ἔργῳ θυμὸν ἔ. 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 445; ἄλλοσ᾽ ὄμμα θητέρᾳ δὲ νοῦν ἔ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 272; τὸν δὲ νοῦν ἐκεῖσ᾽ ἔχει 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 360}}; δεῦρο νοῦν ἔχε **attend to** this, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Or.” 1181}}; πρός τινα or πρός τι τὸν νοῦν ἔ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.22, 7.19; so πρός τινα τὴν γνώμην ἔ. [prev. author] 3.25.}}
**A.II.9** **hold in, stay, keep back**, ἵππους {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.302, 16.712}}; **check, stop**, [τινα] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|23.720, etc. (σχήσω is usually _future_ in this sense, τὸ πεπρωμένον οὐ σιδάρεον σχήσει τεῖχος 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 232, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.820, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 284, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.272, but ἕξω 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.51)}}; χεῖρας ἔχων Ἀχιλῆος **holding** his hands, {{Refs|18.33}}; but οὐ σχήσει χεῖρας **will** not **stay** his hands, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.70; ἔ. [δάκρυον] 16.191}}; ἔ. ὀδύνας **allay, assuage** them, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.848; ἔσχε κῦμα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.451; σιγῇ μῦθον 19.502 (so εἶχε σιγῇ καὶ ἔφραζε οὐδενί 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.93); ἐν φρεσὶ μῦθον 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.445}}; στόμα σῖγα, ἐν ἡσυχίᾳ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 660, “fragment” 773.61 (Lyric poetry); πόδα [prev. author] “IT” 1159}}; πόδα ἔξω or ἐκτός τινος ἔχειν, see at {πούς}:—_passive_, οὖρα σχεθέντα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων ὀξέων παθῶν” 2.5.}}
**A.II.10** **keep away from**, with _genitive_ _of things_, τινὰ ἀγοράων, νεῶν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.275, 13.687; γόων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 375; φόνου 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1005}}: c._infinitive_, ἦ τινα.. σχήσω ἀμυνέμεναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.182}}; **stop, hinder from** doing, τοῦ μὴ καταδῦναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.5.11, compare “HG” 4.8.5; ἔσχον μὴ κτανεῖν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 686, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.158, etc.; μὴ οὐ τάδ᾽ ἐξειπεῖν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 658}}; ὥστε μή.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.5.11; τὸ μὴ ἀδικεῖν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 691, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.101}}: also with _participle_, ἔ. τινὰ βουθυτοῦντα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 888 (troch.); μαργῶντα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1156.}}
**A.II.11** **keep back, withhold** a thing, ὅς οἱ χρήματα εἶχε βίῃ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.231, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 30.14; Ἕκτορ᾽ ἔχει.. οὐδ᾽ ἀπέλυσεν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.115, compare 136}}; αὐτὸς ἔχε **pray keep** it, a civil form of declining, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 270.}}
**A.II.12** **hold in guard, keep safe**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.730}}; of armour, **protect**, {{Refs|22.322.}}
**A.II.13** with predicate, **keep** in a condition or place, εἶχον ἀτρέμας σφέας αὐτούς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.54, compare 53, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 230; ἔ. ἑωυτοὺς κατ᾽ οἴκους 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.79; σαυτὸν ἐκποδών 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 346, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.37; σῖγα νάπη φύλλ᾽ εἶχε 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1085; τοὺς στρατιώτας πολὺν χρόνον πειθομένους ἔ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.2.11.}}
**A.II.14** **hold, consider**, τινὰ θέᾳ ἰκέλαν {{NT+7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 25.3 (uncertain), compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 164; τινὰ ὡς προφήτην NT.Matt.14.5; τινὰ ὅτι προφήτης ἦν NT.Mark.11.32; ἔχε με παρῃτημένον NT.Luke.14.18, compare “POxy.” 292.6 (1st c.AD).}}
**A.III** c._infinitive_, **have means** or **power** to do, **to be able**, with _aorist_ _infinitive_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.217, 16.110, etc.}}: with _present_ _infinitive_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.364, etc.; πόλλ᾽ ἂν λέγειν ἔχοιμι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1047: sometimes with}}_infinitive_ omitted or supplied from context, ἀλλ᾽ οὔ πως ἔτι εἶχε he **could** not, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.354}}; οἷά κ᾽ ἔχωμεν so far as we **be able**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.281; ἐξ οἵων ἔχω 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1379; ὅσον εἶχες 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1452; ὡς ἔχω [prev. author] “Hec.” 614.}}
**A.III.b** **have to face, be obliged**, παθεῖν {{NT+2nd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “adversus Christianos - Abh. Berl. Akad.” 63; εἰ ἕξω βλαβῆναι 2nd c.AD: Astrampsychus Onirocriticus “Oracula” p.5 H.; βάπτισμα ἔχω βαπτισθῆναι NT.Luke.12.50.}}
**A.III.2** after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, οὐκ ἔχω}}, followed by a dependent clause, I **know** not.., οὐκ εἶχον τίς ἂν γενοίμαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 905, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.130; οὐδ᾽ ἔχω πῶς με χρὴ.. ἀφανίσαι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1710; οὐκ ἔχων ὅ τι χρὴ λέγειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.24; οὐκ ἔχω ποῖ πέσω 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 705; ὅπως μολούμεθ᾽ οὐκ ἔχω [prev. author] “OC” 1743}}; the two constructions combined, οὐ γὰρ εἴχομεν οὔτ᾽ ἀντιφωνεῖν οὔθ᾽ ὅπως.. πράξαιμεν {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ant.” 270.}}
**A.IV** _impersonal_ with _accusative_, **there is**.. (as in Mod. Gr.), ἔχει δὲ φυλακτήριον πρὸς τὸ μή σε καταπεσεῖν {{Refs|“PMag.Par.” 1.2505, compare 1262, 1840.}}
**B** _intransitive_, **hold oneself**, i.e. **keep**, so and so, ἔχον [οὕτως], ὥς τε τάλαντα γυνή (i.e. ἔχει) **kept balanced**, like the scales which.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.433}}; ἕξω δ᾽ ὡς ὅτε τις στερεὴ λίθος I **will keep unmoved**, as a stone.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.494, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.679, 24.27; νωλεμέως ἐχέμεν 5.492}}; ἔγχος ἔχ᾽ ἀτρέμας it **kept** still, {{Refs|13.557}}; σχὲς οὗπερ εἶ **keep** where thou art, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1169; ἕξειν κατὰ χώραν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 793, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.42, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 10.10}}; διὰ φυλακῆς ἔχοντες **to keep** on their guard, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.81}}; ἔχε ἠρέμα **keep** still, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 399e, etc.}}; ἔχε δή **stay now**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Prt.” 349e, “Grg.” 460a, etc.; ἔχ᾽ αὐτοῦ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 45.26.}}
**B.2** **hold fast**, οὐδέ οἱ ἔσχεν ὀστέον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.740; compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 11.}}
**B.6**
**B.3** with _genitive_, **keep from**, πολέμου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.112 (compare with IV).}}
**B.4** with Preps., **to be engaged** or **busy**, ἀμφί τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 102 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.2.26, etc.; περί τινας [prev. author] “HG” 7.4.28.}}
**B.II** simply, **be**, ἑκὰς εἶχον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.435; ἔ. κατ᾽ οἴκους 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.39; περὶ πολλῶν ἔ. πρηγμάτων [prev. author] 3.128}}; ἀγῶνα διὰ πάσης ἀγωνίης ἔχοντα **consisting** in.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.91; ἔ. ἐν ἀνάγκαισι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 88 (Lyric poetry); ὅπου συμφορᾶς ἔχεις [prev. author] “El.” 238; ἐκποδὼν ἔχειν [prev. author] “IT” 1226, etc.}}
**B.II.2** frequently with _adverbs_ of manner, εὖ ἔχει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.245, etc.}}; καλῶς ἔχει, κακῶς ἔχει, **it is, is going on** well or ill, see at {καλός, κακός} (but _future_ σχήσειν καλῶς **will turn out** well, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.9, compare 18.45; εὖ σχήσει 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 684)}}; οὕτως.. σχεῖν **to turn out, happen** thus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 39b}}; οὕτως ἔχει so **the case stands**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 110}}; οὕτως ἐχόντων, Latin _cum res ita se habeant_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.2.10; ὡς ὧδ᾽ ἐχόντων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 981; οὕτω χρὴ διὰ στέρνων ἔχειν [prev. author] “Ant.” 639; οὕτως ἔ. περί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.8.7, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.16; πρός τι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.45; τῇδ᾽ ἔ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1336; κοσμίως ἔ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 854; ἥδιον ἔ. πρός τινας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.63}}; ὡς εἶχε just as he **was**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.114; ὥσπερ εἶχε 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.134, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.1.30}}; ὡς ἔχω how I **am**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 610; ὥσπερ ἔχομεν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.30; τἀναντία εἶχεν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.41}}; ἀσφαλέως, ἀναγκαίως ἔχει, ={ἀσφαλές, ἀναγκαῖόν ἐστι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.86, 9.27}}; καλῶς ἔχει **no, I thank you**, see at {καλός}.
**B.II.2.b** with _genitive_ modi, εὖ ἔ. τινός **to be well off for** a thing, **abound in** it; καλῶς ἔ. μέθης **to be** well off for drink, i.e. **to be** pretty well drunk, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.20}}; σπόρου ἀνακῶς ἐ. **to be** busy with sowing, {{Refs|[prev. author] 8.109}}; εὖ ἐ. φρενῶν, σώματος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 462, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 404d; εὖ ὥρας ἔχον χωρίον 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 5.108}}; compare ἥκ; so ὡς ποδῶν εἶχον as fast as they could go, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.116, 9.59; ὡς τάχεος εἶχε ἕκαστος [prev. author] 8.107; ὡς.. τις εὐνοίας ἢ μνήμης ἔχοι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.22; ὡς ὀργῆς ἔχω 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 345, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 313, 857, etc.}}; πῶς ἔχεις δόξη; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 456d; οὕτω τρόπου ἔχεις 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.56; μετρίως ἔ. βίου 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.32; ὑγιεινῶς ἔ. αὐτὸς αὑτοῦ καὶ σωφρόνως 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 571d; οὐκ εὖ σεαυτοῦ τυγχάνεις ἔχων 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 4.11: also with _accusative_, εὖ ἔ. τὸ σῶμα καὶ τὴν ψυχήν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 464a, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 21.7: with _dative_, οὕτως ἐχόντων τούτων τῇ φύσει 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.315; πῶς ἔχετε ταῖς διανοίαις 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 75; τῇ λέξει κακῶς ἔ. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.10.}}
**B.III** of direction, **hold** or **turn** towards, see above {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 11.8.}}
**B.III.2** **stand up, jut out**, κίονες ὑψόσ᾽ ἔχοντες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.38; δι᾽ ὤμου ἔγχος ἔσχεν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.520.}}
**B.III.3** **lead towards**, ὁδοὶ ἐπὶ τὸν ποταμὸν ἔ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.180, compare 191, 2.17}}; ἔ. εἴς τι **to be directed, point** towards, ἔχθρης ἐχούσης ἐς Ἀθηναίους {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.81}}; τὸ ἐς τοὺς Ἀργείους ἔχον **what concerns** them, {{Refs|[prev. author] 6.19; ταῦτα ἐς τὴν ἀπόστασιν ἔχοντα [prev. work] 2}}, etc.; of Place, **extend, reach to**, ἐπ᾽ ὅσον ἔποψις τοῦ ἱροῦ εἶχε {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.64.}}
**B.III.4** ἐπί τινι ἔ. **have** hostile **feelings** to wards.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 6.49, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 987 (Lyric poetry).}}
**B.IV** after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, ἔχω}} as auxiliary, with _aorist_ _participle_ giving a perfect sense, κρύψαντες ἔχουσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 42; ἀποκληΐσας ἔχεις 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.37; ἐγκλῄσασ᾽ ἔχει 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 355, compare “Th.” 706; frequently in 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, θαυμάσας ἔχω “OC” 1140, compare “Ant.” 22, +others}}: also in late Prose, ἀναλώσας ἔχεις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor “Orationes” 18(20).1; ὅς σφε νῦν ἀτιμάσας ἔχει 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 33}}: less frequently with _perfect_ _participle_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 701, “Ph.” 600, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.3.14, 4.7.1}}: rarely with _present_ _participle_, πατρίδα καταστένουσ᾽ ἔχεις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 318 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 10.11.}}
**B.IV.2** _participle_ ἔχων, with _present_, adds a notion of duration to that of present action, τί κυπτάζεις ἔ.; why do you **keep** poking about there? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 509}}; τί δῆτα διατρίβεις ἔ.; why then **keep** wasting time? {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ec.” 1151}}; τί γὰρ ἕστηκ᾽ ἔ.{{Refs|[prev. work] 853, compare “Th.” 473, 852: without _interrogative_, φλυαρεῖς ἔ., ἔ. φλυαρεῖς}}, you **keep** chattering, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 490e, “Euthd.” 295c; κακοῦν ἔχοντ᾽ αὐτὸν ἀποκτιννύναι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.35 (and so possibly ἐνεργεῖ ἔ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1072b23); παίσδεις ἔ. 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 14.8}}: so in later Prose, παίζεις ἔ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Icar.” 24}}; but ῥιπτεῖς ἔ.; do you throw away the prize **when it is in your grasp**? {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 1.443 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}
**C** _middle_, **hold oneself fast, cling closely**, τῷ προσφὺς ἐχόμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.433, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.513, etc.; πρὸς ἀλλήλῃσιν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.329}}: mostly with _genitive_, **hold on by, cling to**, [πέτρης{{Refs 5th c.BC+|] [prev. work] 429; χερσὶν ἀώτου 9.435; βρετέων 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 98 (Lyric poetry); ἑξόμεσθάσου 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 101}}; τῆς πληγῆς ἔχ εται **claps his hand on** the place struck, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.40.}}
**C.2** _metaphorically_, **cleave, cling to**, ἔργου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.11, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.2.19; γεωργίας “BGU” 7.6 (3rd c.AD)}}; τῶν πραγμάτων {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 1.19a}}; βιοτᾶς, ἐλπίδος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 491, “fragment” 409; τῆς αὐτῆς γνώμης 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.140}}; **lay hold on, take advantage of**, τῶν ἀγαθῶν ἔχεο {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 32; προφάσιος ἔχεσθαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.94}}; **fasten upon, attack**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.79}}; **lay claim to**, ἀμφοτέρων τῶν ἐπωνυμιέων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.17}}; **to be zealous for**, [μάχης] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 424; ἀληθείας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 709c; κοινῇ τῆς σωτηρίας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.3.17, etc.}}
**C.3** **come next to, follow closely**,{{Refs|[prev. work] 1.8.4; ἕπεσθαι ἐχομένους ὅτι μάλιστα τῶν ἁρμάτων [prev. author] “Cyr.” 7.1.9}}; of peoples or places, **to be close, border on**, with _genitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.169, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.96, etc.; frequently in}}_participle_, τὴν ἐχομένην [τῶν νεωρίων] στοάν Aen. Tact.{{Refs|11.3}}; οἱ ἐ. the **neighbouring** people, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.134}}; ὁ ἐχόμενος the **next** man, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 22.27}}; of Time, τὸ ἐχόμενον ἔτος the **next** year, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.3; ὁ ἐ. διαλογισμός “PRev.Laws” 16.15 (3rd c.BC)}}; τὰ ἐχόμενα τούτοις **what follows**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 494e (without τούτοις 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.29).}}
**C.4** **depend**, ἔκ τινος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.197, 11.346: with _genitive_, σέο δ᾽ ἕξεται 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.102.}}
**C.4.b** **to be connected with** by etymology, τὸ θύειν τοῦ θυμιᾶν εἴχετο {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Abstinentia - Porphyrii Opuscula” 2.59.}}
**C.5** **pertain to**, ὅσα ἔχεται τῶν αἰσθήσεων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 661b; ἃ διδασκάλων εἴχετο [prev. author] “Prt.” 319e; ὅσα τέχνης ἔχεται [prev. author] “Men.” 94b, etc.: especially in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus in periphrases, τὰ τῶν ὀνειράτων, καρπῶν ἐχόμενα, 1.120, 193}}; ὀρνίθων ἢ ἰχθύων {{Refs|2.77; σιτίων, ἐσθῆτος, 3.25, 66.}}
**C.II** **bear** or **hold for oneself**, κρήδεμνα ἄντα παρειάων σχομένη before her cheeks, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.334}}; ἀσπίδα πρόσθ᾽ ἔσχετο **his** shield, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.294, compare 298, 20.262.}}
**C.III** **maintain oneself, hold one's ground**,{{Refs|12.126; ἔχεο κρατερῶς 16.501.}}
**C.III.2** with _accusative_, **keep off from oneself, repel**, {{Refs|17.639 (unless σχήσεσθαι is _passive_, compare 9.235).}}
**C.IV** **keep oneself back, abstain** or **refrain from**, ἀϋτῆς, μάχης, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|2.98, 3.84; βίης 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.422; ἐχώμεθα δηϊοτῆτος ἐκ βελέων 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.129; τῆς ἀγωγῆς 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.85; τῆς τιμωρίης [prev. author] 7.169; τῶν ἀθίκτων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 891 (Lyric poetry, assuming variant)}}: c._infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.328; οὐκ ἂν ἐσχόμην τὸ μὴ ἀποκλῇσαι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1387}}; κακῶν ἄπο χεῖρας ἔχεσθαι **to keep one's** hands from ill, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.316; Μενέλεω σχέσθαι χέρα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 174: _absolutely_, σχέο, σχέσθε}}, **hold! cease**! {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.379, 22.416.}}
**C.V** _passive_ of ἔχω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 1, ἐπὶ ξυροῦ ἀκμῆς ἔχεται ἡμῖν τὰ πρήγματα}} **are balanced** on.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.11.}}

2193:
** ἕως**,
**B**_Epic dialect_ **εἵως**, **ἧος** (see. entry near the end), _Doric dialect_ **ἇς**, _Aeolic dialect_ **ἆς** (which see), _Boeotian dialect_ **ἇς** {{Refs|“IG” 7.3303, +others}}, and **ἅως** {{Refs|[prev. work]2228, 3315.}} Relat. Particle, expressing the point of Time up to which an action goes, with reference to the end of the action, **until, till**; or to its continuance, **while**:
**B.I** **until, till**,
**B.I.1** with Ind., of a fact in past time, θῦνε διὰ προμάχων, ἧος φίλον ὤλεσε θυμόν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.342, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.123; ἕ. ἀπώλεσέν τε καὐτὸς ἐξαπώλετο 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 236, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 428, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 155c, etc.; for πρίν, μὴ πρότερον ἀπελθεῖν ἕως ἀποκατέστησε τὰ πράγματα 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 27.4: with}}_imperfect_ with ἄν in apodosi, of an unaccomplished action, ἡδέως ἂν Καλλικλεῖ διελεγόμην, ἕ. ἀπέδωκα I would have gone on conversing **till** I had.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 506b, compare “Cra.” 396c.}}
**B.I.2** ἕ. ἄν or κε with Subj. (mostly of _aorist_), of an event at an uncertain future time, μαχήσομαι.. ἧός κε τέλος πολέμοιο κιχείω **till** I find, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.291, compare 24.183, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 810, etc.: ἄν}} is sometimes omitted in Trag., ἕ. μάθῃς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 555; ἕ. κληθῇ [prev. author] “Tr.” 148; ἕ. ἀνῇ τὸ πῆμα [prev. author] “Ph.” 764}}: so frequently in later Gr., {{NT+2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 18.10 (2nd c.BC), “PGrenf.” 2.38.16 (1st c.BC), NT.Mark.14.32, Vett. Val. 68.18, etc.; ἕ. οὗ γένηται 1st c.BC: Geminus Astronomicus 8.32.}}
**B.I.3** ἕ. with Opt. (mostly of _aorist_), relating to an event future in relation to past time, ὦρσε.. Βορέην, ἧος ὃ Φαιήκεσσι.. μιγείη caused it to blow, **till** he should reach.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.386, compare 9.376, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 766, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 59d; ἕως δέοι βοηθεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.102, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.25: ἄν}} or κε is added to the Opt. (not to ἕως), if the event is represented as conditional, ἕ. κ᾽ ἀπὸ πάντα δοθείη **till** (if possible) all things should be given back, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.78; οὐκ [ἂν] ἀποκρίναιο, ἕ. ἂν σκέψαιο 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 101d, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 687 codices, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 17.15, “IG” 22.1328 (2nd c.BC).}}
**B.I.3.b** in indirect speech, ἔδωκεν.. ἕ. ἀνὴρ εἶναι δοκιμασθείην {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.5.}}
**B.I.3.c** by assimilation to an _optative_ with ἄν, [λόγον] ἂν διδοίης ἕ. ἔλθοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 101d.}}
**B.I.4** with _subjunctive_ or _optative_, expressing purpose, **in order that**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.800, 6.80, 19.367; πορεύου εἰς Διονυσιάδα.. ἕως τὸν ἐκεῖ ἐλαιῶνα ποτίσῃς “PFay.” 118.12 (2nd c.AD)}}; σπούδασον ἕως οὗ ἀγοράσῃ κτλ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 113.25 (2nd c.AD); χρυσίον ἐδανισάμην ἕως ὅτε δυνηθῶ ἀγοράσαι [prev. work] 130.13 (6th c.AD).}}
**B.I.5** with Inf. in indirect speech, ἐντειλάμενος διεκπλέειν ἕ... ἀπικνέεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.42}}: otherwise only in later Gr., ἕ. ἐλθεῖν ἐς.. [{{LXX+1st c.BC+|LXX.Gen.10.19, compare “PLond.” 1.131r251 (1st c.AD), 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 9.4 (variant), Anonymus cited in Suid. see at {ἰλυσπώμενον}.}}]
**B.I.6** with _adverbs_ of Time and Place, ἕ. ὅτε **till the time when**, with _indicative_, variant for{ἔστε} in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.1.25}}; ἕ. οὗ, _falsa lectio_ for{ἐς οὗ}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.143}}: frequently in later Gr., Gem.[same place], {{NT|NT.Matt.1.25, etc.; ἕ. ὅτου [prev. work] 5.25, etc.}}; ἕ. πότ; **how long**{{LXX+NT|? [prev. work] 17.17, NT.John.10.24; ἕ. τότε LXX.Neh.2.16}}; ἕ. ὀψέ **till** late, _falsa lectio_ for{ἐς ὀψέ}, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.108; ἕ. ἄρτι NT.1John.2.9}}; ἕ. ὧδε **as far as** this place, {{NT|NT.Luke.23.5.}}
**B.I.6.b** with Preps., of Time, ἕ. πρὸς καλὸν ἑῷον ἀστέρα{{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” 5.200}}; of Place, ἕ. εἰς τὸν χάρακα {{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.11.14; ἕ. πρὸς τὸν Καύκασον 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 2.43; ἕ. ἐπὶ τὴν θάλασσαν NT.Act.17.14.}}
**B.II** as _preposition_,
**B.II.1** of Time, with _genitive_, **until**, ἕως τοῦ ἀποτεῖσαι **until** he has made payment, Legal cited in {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.42, cf. LXX.Gen.3.19, etc.; ἕ. τελειώσεως 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 2p.38U.; ἕ. ὡρισμένων χρόνων 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 1.7}}; ἕ. τινός **for** a time, {{NT+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Parthenius Mythographus 9.2, etc.; ἕ. τοῦ νῦν NT.Matt.24.21; ἕ. Ἰωάννου [prev. work] 11.13.}}
**B.II.1.b** of Place, ἕ. τοῦ γενέσθαι.. **up to the point where..** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 668b2, compare “HA” 630b27, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.36.1}}; **as far as**, ἕ. Σάρδεων {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Ath.Mitt.” 44.25 (Samos, 3rd c.BC)}}; ἕ. τοῦ Ἀρσινοΐτου νομοῦ {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 33.5 (2nd c.BC)}}; ἕ. Φοινίκης {{LXX+NT|NT.Act.11.19: so with _genitive_ _person_, ἦλθον ἕ. αὐτοῦ NT.Luke.4.42, cf. LXX.4Ki.4.22.}}
**B.II.1.c** of Number or Degree, ἕ. τριῶν πλοίων Docum. cited in {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.106; διδόναι ἕ. ταλάντων ἑκατόν LXX.1Esd.8.19(21)}}; οὐκ ἔστιν ἕ. ἑνός{{LXX+2nd c.BC+|LXX.Psa.13.3; οὐκ ἔχομεν ἕ. τῆς τροφῆς τῶν κτηνῶν “PTeb.” 56.7 (2nd c.BC)}}; ἐᾶτε ἕ. τούτου {{NT+3rd c.BC+|NT.Luke.22.51; μαχοῦμαι ἕ. ζωῆς καὶ θανάτου “OGI” 266.29 (Pergam., 3rd c.BC)}}; ἕ. μέθης {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Cornutus Philosophus “de Natura Deorum” 30.}}
**B.II.2** rarely with _accusative_, ἕ. πρωΐ {{LXX+1st c.AD+|LXX.Judg.19.25; ἕ. μεσημβρίαν “PLond.” 1.131r346, 515 (1st c.AD)}}; ἕ. τὸ βωμῷ **down to** the word “βωμῷ”, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Scholia 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.111.}}
**B.III** **while, so long as**, with _indicative_, ἧος ἐνὶ Τροίῃ πολεμίζομεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.315, compare 17.358, 390}}; ἕ. δ᾽ ἔτ᾽ ἔμφρων εἰμί {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 1026, compare “Pers.” 710 (troch.)}}; ἕ. ἔτι ἐλπὶς [ἦν] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.40; ἕ. ἔτινέος εἶ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 135d: in this sense answered in apodosi by τῆος, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.90, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.41}}; by τόφρα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.327, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.15; by τόφρα δέ, 10.507}}; by δέ alone,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|1.193, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.120 codices}}
**B.III.b** ἕ. ἄν c._subjunctive_, when the whole action is future, οὔ μοι.. ἐλπίς, ἕ. ἂν αἴθῃ πῦρ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1435; λέγειν τε χρὴ καὶ ἐρωτᾶν, ἕως ἂν ἐῶσιν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 85b; οὐδὲν ἔστ᾽ αὐτῷ βεβαίως ἔχειν ἕ. ἂν ὑμεῖς δημοκρατῆσθε 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 10.13.}}
**B.III.c** ἕως with _optative_ in a Conditional relative clause, φήσομεν μηδὲν ἂν μεῖζον μηδὲ ἔλαττον γενέσθαι ἕ. ἴσον εἴη αὐτὸ ἐαυτῷ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 155a.}}
**B** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus sometimes Demonstr.,= τέως}}, **for a time**, ἧος μὲν.. ὄρνυον· αὐτὰρ ἐπεὶ.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.141; ἧος μὲν ἀπείλει.. · ἀλλ᾽ ὅτε δὴ.. 13.143, compare 17.727, 730, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.148; ἧος μὲν.. ἕποντο.. αὐτὰρ ἐπεὶ.. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.277}}; **all that time**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.126, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.74. (ἕως}}, as iambus, only once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.78}}; as a _monosyllable_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.727, uncertain reading in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.148}}; when the first syllable is to be long codices {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus have εἵως or ἕως (never εἷος or ἧος, Ludwich “WkP” 1890.512}}, except ειος variant {{Refs 8th c.BC+|(“PFay.” 160) in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.41), 3.291, 11.342, +others; εἵως (or ἕως) is found even when the metre requires a trochee, 1.193, +others}}; comparison of _Doric dialect_ ἇς (from *ἇος) with _Attic dialect_-_Ionic dialect_ ἕως points to early _Ionic dialect_ *ἧος (cf. Sanskrit _yāvat_ 'as great as, as long as, until') and this should probably be restored in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}; compare τέως.)

2198:
** ζῶ**
(_contraction_ from ζώω: **ζάω** only in Grammars, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“Etymologicum Magnum” 410.38), ζῇς (4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus “Scholia in Theodosii Canones” 2.28), ζῇ, ζῆτε (but ζῆς, ζῆ _accusative_ to Anonymus cited in “Etymologicum Magnum” 410.48, Sophronius cited in 4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus “Scholia in Theodosii Canones” 2.416)}}; _imperative_ ζῆ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 167, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 699, ζῆθι (as if from ζῆμι,compare “Etymologicum Magnum” [same place]) 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 11 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 191, “Anthologia Graeca” 10.43, σύ-ζηθι 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus cited in “Etymologicum Genuinum”}} see at {ζῆ}; _optative_ ζῴη; _infinitive_ ζῆν: _imperfect_ ἔζων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 323, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1072}}; ἔζην in most codices of {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.7 is a form suggested by ἔζης, ἔζη}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἔζων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 709, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 679c}}: _future_ ζήσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 263, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 465d, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 186, [5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus] 267, ζήσομαι 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ φύσιος παιδίου” 30, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.82, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1327b5}}: _aorist 1_ ἔζησα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προγνωστικόν” 1, “Anthologia Graeca” 7.470 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.786a, etc.}}: _perfect_ ἔζηκα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1048b25, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 5.68, etc.: but in _Attic dialect_}}_aorist_ and _perfect_ are mostly supplied from βιόω.- Exc. _participle_ ζῶντος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.88, 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} always uses the _Epic dialect_ _Ionic dialect_ Lyric poetry _present_ **ζώω** (also in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.25, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.46, +others, 5th c.BC: Diogenes Apolloniates Philosophus 4, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 2.29, “IG” 12(8).600.9 (from Thasos), and Trag. (in Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 157, “OC” 1213, compare “BCH” 47.95 (from Cavalla), “Bull.Soc.Arch.Bulg.” 7.13 (from Macedonia)}}; _subjunctive_ ζώῃ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 12(8).262.12 (Thasos, 5th c.BC),compare “Schwyzer” 339, +others (Delph.)}}, _contraction_ ζῷ {{Refs|“Berl.Sitzb.” 1927.161 (from Cyrene)}}; Cretan dialect **δώω** {{Refs|“Leg.Gort.” 4.21, +others)}}; _infinitive_ ζωέμεναι, -έμεν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.140, 24.436}}: _imperfect_ ἔζωον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|22.245, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 112, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.112}}; _Ionic dialect_ ζώεσκον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 90, 2nd c.BC: Bion Bucolicus 1.30:}}_aorist 1_ ἔζωσα (ἐπ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.120}}; _infinitive_ ζῶσαι {{Refs|“IG” 11(4).1299 (from Delos)}}: _perfect_ _participle_ ἐζωκότα {{Refs|“BMus.Inscription” 1009 (from Cyzicus)}}; _infinitive_ ζόειν {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC(?): Semonides Iambographus 1.17}}: _imperfect_ ζόεν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 13.21 (3rd c.BC: Theodoridas Epigrammaticus)}}. (Root **g[uglide]iē-, g[uglide]iō-** also in βίος and ὑγιής (which see).)
**I** properly of animal life, **live**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (see. below), etc.}}; also of plants, τὸ ζῆν κοινὸν εἶναι φαίνεται καὶ τοῖς φυτοῖς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1097b33}}; ἐλέγχιστε ζωόντων vilest **of living men**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.72; ζώειν καὶ ὁρᾶν φάος ἠελίοιο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.558; ζῶντος καὶ ἐπὶ χθονὶ δερκομένοιο 1.88, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.439; ζῶν καὶ βλέπων 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 677; ζώει τε καὶ ἔστιν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.263; ζώντων καὶ ὄντων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.72; τοῦ εἶναί τε καὶ ζῆν ἕνεκα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 369d; ζῶσα πόλις καὶ ἐγρηγορυῖα [prev. author] “Lg.” 809d; ζῶν καὶ ἔμψυχος [prev. author] “Phdr.” 276a}}; ῥεῖα ζώοντες **living** at ease, of the gods, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.138, +others}}; ζῶν κατακαυθῆναι to be burnt **alive**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.86: with _accusative_ temporary, ζ. ἤματα πάντα “hymnus ad Venerem” 221, etc.; ὀλίγα ἔτεα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.22: with _dative_ modi, δμῶες.. ἄλλα τε πολλὰ οἷσίν τ᾽ εὖ ζώουσι}} whereby men **live** in comfort, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.423, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 60.5; κοράκων πονηρίᾳ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 868}}; ἐπὶ τοῖς αἰσχίστοις ἔργοις, ἐπὶ τοῖς παροῦσιν ἀγαθοῖς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.100, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 10.18}}; also ζῆν ἀπό τινος **to live on** a thing, {{NT+6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1156, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.216, 2.36, 4.22, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 850, etc.; ἔκ τινος [prev. author] “Ec.” 591, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 57.36, NT.1Cor.9.14}}: with _participle_, ζῆν συκοφαντῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.99; ἐργαζόμενοι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1292b27: with dative commodi, ζῆν ἑαυτῷ}} **for** oneself, uncertain reading in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 646, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 470, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 507}}; τὸ ζῆν,= ζωή, {{NT+5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 681, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 77d (without _Article_ εἰς ἕτερον ζ. [prev. author] “Ax.” 365d); διὰ παντὸς τοῦ ζῆν NT.Heb.2.15}}; also, **a living**, τὸ ζ. οὐκ ἔχομεν {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“OGI” 515.57 (Mylasa, 3rd c.AD)}}; ζήτω ὁ βασιλεύς **long live** the king, {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.10.24}}; βασιλεῦ, εἰς τὸν αἰῶνα ζῆθι{{LXX|LXX.Dan.3.9}}; asseverations, ζῶ ἐγώ, καὶ ζῶν τὸ ὄνομά μου, καί.. {{LXX|LXX.Num.14.21}}; ζῇ κύριος, εἰ.., ὅτι..,{{LXX|LXX.1Ki.19.6, 29.6; ζῇ ἡ ψυχή σου, εἰ οἶδα [prev. work] 17.55.}}
**I.2** ={βιόω}, **live, pass one's life**, with _accusative_ _cognate_, ζώεις δ᾽ ἀγαθὸν βίον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.491; ζ. βίον μοχθηρόν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 599, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 249, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 506, etc.; ζόην τὴν αὐτήν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.112, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 344e; τὸν βίον ἀσφαλῶς 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 213.5; ἥδιστον ἀνθρώπων βίον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 583.4; νυμφίων βίον 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 161; also ζ. ἀβλαβεῖ βίῳ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 650, compare “Tr.” 168; εὖ ζῆν [prev. author] “Ph.” 505; κακῶς [prev. author] “OC” 799; ζ. δοῦλος [prev. author] “OT” 410}}; ἐκ τῶν ἄλλων ὧν ἔζης from the other **acts of your life**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.134; ποιεῖσθαι φθόνον ἐξ ὧν ζῇς [prev. work]196.}}
**I.3** _aorist 1_ ἔζησα, causal, **quicken**, ἐν τῇ ὁδῷ σου ζῆσόν με {{LXX|LXX.Psa.118(119).37, +others}}
**II** **live** in the fullest sense, δι᾽ ὧν ζῆν ἐπιστάμεθα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.3.11, etc.; βιοὺς μὲν ἔτη τόσα, ζήσας δὲ ἔτη ἑπτά 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 69.19}}; in religious or mystical sense, {{NT|NT.Rom.7.9, +others}}, cf. Ramsay **Cilies and Bishoprics** {{Refs|2p.565 (Phryg.)}}; θεὸς ζῶν {{LXX|LXX.Deu.5.26(23), etc.}}: frequently _metaphorically_ of things, **to be in full vigour**, ὄλβος ζώει μάσσων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 3.5; ἄτης θύελλαι ζῶσι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 819; ζῶντι χρώμενος ποδί 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 790; [μαντεῖα] αἰεὶ ζῶντα περιποτᾶται [prev. author] “OT” 482}}; ἀεὶ ζῇ ταῦτα [νόμιμα] {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ant.” 457}}; τὰς ξυμφορὰς τῶν βουλευμάτων ζώσας μάλιστα **have** most **living power**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “OT” 45; λόγια ζῶντα NT.Act.7.38; χρόνῳ τῷ ζῶντι καὶ παρόντι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1169}}; ζῶσα φλόξ **living** fire, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 8}}; ὕδωρ ζῶν **spring** water, {{LXX+NT+1st c.BC+|LXX.Num.5.17 (and _metaphorically_, NT.John.4.10); ζώσης φωνῆς 1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Atticum” 2.12.2.}}
** ζῶ**
(_contraction_ from ζώω: ζάω only in Grammars, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“Etymologicum Magnum” 410.38), ζῇς (4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus “Scholia in Theodosii Canones” 2.28), ζῇ, ζῆτε (but ζῆς, ζῆ _accusative_ to Anonymus cited in “Etymologicum Magnum” 410.48, Sophronius cited in 4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus “Scholia in Theodosii Canones” 2.416)}}; _imperative_ ζῆ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 167, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 699, ζῆθι (as if from ζῆμι,compare “Etymologicum Magnum” [same place]) 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 11 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 191, “Anthologia Graeca” 10.43, σύ-ζηθι 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus cited in “Etymologicum Genuinum”}} see at {ζῆ}; _optative_ ζῴη; _infinitive_ ζῆν: _imperfect_ ἔζων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 323, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1072}}; ἔζην in most codices of {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.7 is a form suggested by ἔζης, ἔζη}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἔζων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 709, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 679c}}: _future_ ζήσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 263, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 465d, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 186, [5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus] 267, ζήσομαι 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ φύσιος παιδίου” 30, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.82, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1327b5}}: _aorist 1_ ἔζησα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προγνωστικόν” 1, “Anthologia Graeca” 7.470 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.786a, etc.}}: _perfect_ ἔζηκα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1048b25, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 5.68, etc.: but in _Attic dialect_}}_aorist_ and _perfect_ are mostly supplied from βιόω.- Exc. _participle_ ζῶντος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.88, 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} always uses the _Epic dialect_ _Ionic dialect_ Lyric poetry _present_ **ζώω** (also in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.25, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.46, +others, 5th c.BC: Diogenes Apolloniates Philosophus 4, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 2.29, “IG” 12(8).600.9 (from Thasos), and Trag. (in Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 157, “OC” 1213, compare “BCH” 47.95 (from Cavalla), “Bull.Soc.Arch.Bulg.” 7.13 (from Macedonia)}}; _subjunctive_ ζώῃ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 12(8).262.12 (Thasos, 5th c.BC),compare “Schwyzer” 339, +others (Delph.)}}, _contraction_ ζῷ {{Refs|“Berl.Sitzb.” 1927.161 (from Cyrene)}}; Cretan dialect **δώω** {{Refs|“Leg.Gort.” 4.21, +others)}}; _infinitive_ ζωέμεναι, -έμεν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.140, 24.436}}: _imperfect_ ἔζωον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|22.245, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 112, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.112}}; _Ionic dialect_ ζώεσκον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 90, 2nd c.BC: Bion Bucolicus 1.30:}}_aorist 1_ ἔζωσα (ἐπ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.120}}; _infinitive_ ζῶσαι {{Refs|“IG” 11(4).1299 (from Delos)}}: _perfect_ _participle_ ἐζωκότα {{Refs|“BMus.Inscription” 1009 (from Cyzicus)}}; _infinitive_ ζόειν {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC(?): Semonides Iambographus 1.17}}: _imperfect_ ζόεν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 13.21 (3rd c.BC: Theodoridas Epigrammaticus)}}. (Root **g[uglide]iē-, g[uglide]iō-** also in βίος and ὑγιής (which see).)
**I** properly of animal life, **live**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (see. below), etc.}}; also of plants, τὸ ζῆν κοινὸν εἶναι φαίνεται καὶ τοῖς φυτοῖς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1097b33}}; ἐλέγχιστε ζωόντων vilest **of living men**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.72; ζώειν καὶ ὁρᾶν φάος ἠελίοιο 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.558; ζῶντος καὶ ἐπὶ χθονὶ δερκομένοιο 1.88, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.439; ζῶν καὶ βλέπων 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 677; ζώει τε καὶ ἔστιν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.263; ζώντων καὶ ὄντων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.72; τοῦ εἶναί τε καὶ ζῆν ἕνεκα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 369d; ζῶσα πόλις καὶ ἐγρηγορυῖα [prev. author] “Lg.” 809d; ζῶν καὶ ἔμψυχος [prev. author] “Phdr.” 276a}}; ῥεῖα ζώοντες **living** at ease, of the gods, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.138, +others}}; ζῶν κατακαυθῆναι to be burnt **alive**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.86: with _accusative_ temporary, ζ. ἤματα πάντα “hymnus ad Venerem” 221, etc.; ὀλίγα ἔτεα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.22: with _dative_ modi, δμῶες.. ἄλλα τε πολλὰ οἷσίν τ᾽ εὖ ζώουσι}} whereby men **live** in comfort, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.423, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 60.5; κοράκων πονηρίᾳ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 868}}; ἐπὶ τοῖς αἰσχίστοις ἔργοις, ἐπὶ τοῖς παροῦσιν ἀγαθοῖς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.100, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 10.18}}; also ζῆν ἀπό τινος **to live on** a thing, {{NT+6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1156, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.216, 2.36, 4.22, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 850, etc.; ἔκ τινος [prev. author] “Ec.” 591, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 57.36, NT.1Cor.9.14}}: with _participle_, ζῆν συκοφαντῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.99; ἐργαζόμενοι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1292b27: with dative commodi, ζῆν ἑαυτῷ}} **for** oneself, uncertain reading in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 646, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 470, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 507}}; τὸ ζῆν,= ζωή, {{NT+5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 681, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 77d (without _Article_ εἰς ἕτερον ζ. [prev. author] “Ax.” 365d); διὰ παντὸς τοῦ ζῆν NT.Heb.2.15}}; also, **a living**, τὸ ζ. οὐκ ἔχομεν {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“OGI” 515.57 (Mylasa, 3rd c.AD)}}; ζήτω ὁ βασιλεύς **long live** the king, {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.10.24}}; βασιλεῦ, εἰς τὸν αἰῶνα ζῆθι{{LXX|LXX.Dan.3.9}}; asseverations, ζῶ ἐγώ, καὶ ζῶν τὸ ὄνομά μου, καί.. {{LXX|LXX.Num.14.21}}; ζῇ κύριος, εἰ.., ὅτι..,{{LXX|LXX.1Ki.19.6, 29.6; ζῇ ἡ ψυχή σου, εἰ οἶδα [prev. work] 17.55.}}
